messages.content:
`pictureAttributes` (type `HTMLAttributes<'picture'>`) is an object of attributes added to the outer `<picture>` tag of the `<Picture />` component's output. Attributes passed directly to `<Picture />` apply to the inner `<img>` (except image transformation props), so use `pictureAttributes` for the wrapper element:

```astro
---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import myImage from "../my_image.png";
---
<Picture
  src={myImage}
  alt="A description of my image."
  pictureAttributes={{ style: "background-color: red;" }}
/>
```

This renders `<picture style="background-color: red;">…</picture>`.

In middleware, use `getActionContext()` from `astro:actions` to obtain the pending action, invoke its `handler()`, and persist the resulting `SafeResult` by first passing it through `serializeActionResult()` before `setActionResult()`:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';
import { getActionContext } from 'astro:actions';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const { action, setActionResult, serializeActionResult } = getActionContext(context);
  if (action?.calledFrom === 'form') {
    const result = await action.handler();
    setActionResult(action.name, serializeActionResult(result));
  }
  return next();
});
```

`serializeActionResult()` converts the result to JSON so non-JSON return values (`Map`, `Date`) and the `ActionError` object survive persistence; `deserializeActionResult()` reverses it later to recover `data`/`error`. The middleware must still return a `Response` — returning anything else throws `MiddlewareNotAResponse` in development.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/

Returning `undefined` from `trailingSlash()` simply means no trailing-slash normalization redirect is needed, and your pipeline continues to the next handler (redirects, actions, middleware/pages, etc.). Its type is `(state: FetchState) => Response | undefined` — it synchronously returns a redirect `Response` only when the request pathname must be normalized per your `trailingSlash` config:

```ts
const slashRedirect = trailingSlash(state);
if (slashRedirect) return slashRedirect;
```

This is the normal, expected outcome for already-conforming paths, not an error.

`i18n()` post-processes a rendered response against your i18n config: it handles locale redirects, 404s for invalid locales, and fallback routing. Call it after rendering (e.g., after `pages()`) in the fetch pipeline.

```ts
import { FetchState, i18n, middleware, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const response = await middleware(state, (s) => pages(s));
    return i18n(state, response);
  },
};
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-fetch/

Call `sessions(state)` from `astro/fetch` early in your fetch pipeline. It registers the session provider; sessions are created lazily when your code accesses `ctx.session` and persisted automatically at the end of the request.

```ts
import { FetchState, sessions, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    await sessions(state);
    return pages(state);
  },
};
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-fetch/

You must define build-time content collections (like Markdown blogs) in `src/content.config.ts` and live collections (like real-time products) in `src/live.config.ts`. Each config file exports its own `collections` object. Do not mix them in the same file or export.

Example structure:

**src/content.config.ts**
```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
const blog = defineCollection({ /* ... */ });
export const collections = { blog };
```

**src/live.config.ts**
```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const products = defineLiveCollection({ /* ... */ });
export const collections = { products };
```

Use `getCollection()`/`getEntry()` for content and `getLiveCollection()`/`getLiveEntry()` for live data.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#live-content-collections

You can access the `cacheHint` property from a `LiveDataEntryResult` object returned by `getLiveEntry()`.

Example:

```astro
---
import { getLiveEntry } from "astro:content";
const { cacheHint } = await getLiveEntry("products", Astro.params.id);
if (cacheHint) {
  Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
}
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#livedataentryresult

The `envField.number()` helper supports number-specific validation properties like `gt`, `lt`, `int`, which are not available on `envField.string()`. The `gt` property ensures a number is greater than a specified value, while `min` ensures it's greater than or equal to a value. Use `gt` when you need strict inequality (e.g., port > 1024), and `min` when the minimum value is inclusive (e.g., a count >= 0).

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      PORT: envField.number({
        context: "server",
        access: "public",
        gt: 1024,
        lt: 65536,
      }),
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/

Inside middleware, call `getActionContext()` (imported from `astro:actions`) to get the `action` object for the incoming request, then compare `action.calledFrom` to `'form'`:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';
import { getActionContext } from 'astro:actions';

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const { action, setActionResult, serializeActionResult } = getActionContext(context);
  if (action?.calledFrom === 'form') {
    const result = await action.handler();
    setActionResult(action.name, serializeActionResult(result));
  }
  return next();
});
```

When `calledFrom === 'form'`, the action was invoked by an HTML `<form method="POST" action={actions.x}>` submission; otherwise it arrived via an RPC call from client-side `actions.x(...)` and you should not run the form persistence path. The `action` object also carries the action `name` and `handler`. Note that calling an action directly from a server-rendered page or endpoint without `Astro.callAction()` throws `ActionCalledFromServerError`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Set `compressHTML` in `astro.config.mjs` with `defineConfig`; it accepts `boolean | "jsx"` and defaults to `'jsx'` in Astro 7.

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  compressHTML: true
});
```

Since v7.0 the default `'jsx'` applies JSX whitespace rules (stripping whitespace between elements), so use `true` for the previous HTML-aware lossless compression and `false` to preserve all whitespace. The value also applies to development output and is readable via `compressHTML` from `astro:config/client`.

Just call `app.render(request)` with the request: it calls the Astro page that matches the request and returns a `Response`, and this also works for API routes that do not render pages, so no `app.match()` call is needed.

```js
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  const response = await app.render(request);
  return response;
};
```

Astro's router dispatches the matching endpoint function (`GET`, `POST`, `ALL`, etc.) for you; if no HTTP method matches, it redirects to your site's 404 page, and `GET` also covers `HEAD` by stripping the body. Unmatched requests are handled by your `404.astro` file. Since Astro 5.0, any extra arguments (e.g. `routeData`, `locals`) are passed as a single options object to `app.render()`.

Use `astro/hono` (added in `astro@7.0.0`): it exports Astro's handlers as Hono middleware functions, so you can mix them with any Hono middleware from the ecosystem by exporting a Hono app from `src/fetch.ts`:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';

const app = new Hono();
app.use(logger());
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Register Astro handlers with `app.use()` in your desired order, and add your own Hono middleware (e.g. `logger()`) or path-scoped guards such as `app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth)` alongside them.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

`actions()` in the `astro/fetch` pipeline handles both kinds of action requests server-side, but they differ in what comes back: RPC actions (client calls like `await actions.likePost({ postId })`, returning a Devalue-serialized `{ data, error }` object) produce a `Response`, while form actions and non-action requests produce `undefined`. Structure your route handler to branch on that:

```ts
import { actions, FetchState } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const actionResponse = await actions(state);
    if (actionResponse) return actionResponse;
    // otherwise continue to page rendering...
  },
};
```

For form submissions, continue to rendering and read the outcome in the page with `Astro.getActionResult(actions.newsletter)`, checking `result?.error` (or `isInputError()` for per-field messages) before using `data`.

Use `astro/hono` instead of `astro/fetch` when you prefer Hono as your routing framework: both modules export the same handler names, but `astro/hono` returns each handler as a Hono middleware function, so you can register Astro's handlers with `app.use()` and mix them freely with Hono ecosystem middleware (e.g. `hono/logger`) and with your own route guards. With `astro/fetch` you get the plain handlers instead, without that Hono integration.

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

Create the app with `createApp()` from `astro/app/entrypoint`, then pass it directly to `new StaticPaths(app)`:

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';
import { StaticPaths } from 'astro:static-paths';

const app = createApp();
const staticPaths = new StaticPaths(app);

export const handler = (event, context) => {
  // do something with `staticPaths`
};
```

The app instance supplies the SSR manifest, route cache, and route component access the constructor needs.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/

To build a minimal server entrypoint for a custom Astro adapter, import `createApp` from `"astro/app/entrypoint"`, use it to create an app instance, and handle incoming requests by calling `app.render(request)` to get a `Response`. Return that response in your platform's fetch handler.

Example:

```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";

const app = createApp();

export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  return await app.render(request);
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/

In your middleware file — `src/middleware.js` (or `src/middleware.ts`) — inside an `onRequest` handler created with `defineMiddleware` from `astro:middleware`. `notFound` is imported from the `astro:i18n` virtual module and is only available when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"` (added in `astro@4.6.0`):

```js
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) return pathNotFound;
  return next();
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-i18n/

`cacheHint` is optional metadata your live loader returns alongside entries (in `LiveDataCollection` or `LiveDataEntry`) to assist response caching: `tags` (string identifiers for fine-grained invalidation, merged across entries) and `lastModified` (a `Date`, most recent wins). Added in `astro@6.0.0`. It does not cache anything by itself — you apply it manually. With a configured cache provider, pass it to `Astro.cache.set()`; otherwise set headers yourself:

```astro
---
export const prerender = false;
import { getLiveEntry } from 'astro:content';
const { entry, error, cacheHint } = await getLiveEntry('products', Astro.params.id);
if (error) return Astro.redirect('/404');
if (cacheHint?.tags) Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Tag', cacheHint.tags.join(','));
if (cacheHint?.lastModified) Astro.response.headers.set('Last-Modified', cacheHint.lastModified.toUTCString());
---
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

The `sequence()` function in Astro middleware chains multiple middleware functions to execute them in a specified order. It takes middleware handler functions as arguments and runs them sequentially, allowing you to compose complex request processing logic.

Example:
```js
import { sequence } from "astro:middleware";

async function validation(_, next) {
  console.log("validation request");
  const response = await next();
  console.log("validation response");
  return response;
}

async function auth(_, next) {
  console.log("auth request");
  const response = await next();
  console.log("auth response");
  return response;
}

export const onRequest = sequence(validation, auth);
```

This executes `validation` first, then `auth`, and finally returns the response back through each middleware in reverse order.

To integrate a deploy host that doesn’t use the standard `Request`/`Response` API, create a server entrypoint that converts your host’s request format into a `Request` object and wraps the adapter’s response in your host’s expected format.

Use `astro/app/entrypoint` to build an app instance with `createApp()`, then handle conversion logic inside your handler function. For example:

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';

const app = createApp();

export async function handler(event, context) {
  // Convert host request format to standard Request object
  const request = new Request(`https://${context.domain}${event.path}`, {
    method: event.method,
    headers: event.headers,
    body: event.body,
  });

  // Render with Astro app
  const response = await app.render(request);

  // Wrap Astro's Response in host-specific format
  return context.respond(response);
}
```

This approach allows your adapter to handle non-standard APIs while leveraging Astro’s routing and rendering.

`experimental_getFontFileURL()` (from `astro:assets`, added in `astro@6.2.0`) resolves a font file URL obtained from `fontData` into a usable URL: `(url: string, requestUrl?: URL) => Promise<string>`.

```js
import { fontData, experimental_getFontFileURL } from "astro:assets";
const fontPath = fontData["--font-roboto"][0]?.src[0]?.url;
const url = experimental_getFontFileURL(fontPath);
const buffer = await fetch(url).then((res) => res.arrayBuffer());
```

On an on-demand rendered route, the request URL must be passed as the second argument — e.g. `experimental_getFontFileURL(fontPath, context.url)` inside an API route where `export const prerender = false;` (not needed in `'server'` mode). Without it, Astro throws "experimental_getFontFileURL() requires the request URL with on-demand rendering." An invalid path throws "Font file URL not found."

`isActionError()` (type `(error?: unknown) => boolean`) checks whether an action's `handler()` threw an `ActionError`, narrowing a generic `error` to `ActionError` so you can inspect its `code` and `message`:

```js
import { isActionError, actions } from 'astro:actions';
const { data, error } = await actions.myAction({ /* ... */ });
if (isActionError(error)) {
  console.log(error.code);
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/

To define a live collection named `products` that fetches data from an external API, create a `src/live.config.ts` file and use `defineLiveCollection()` with a custom loader.

Example:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { apiLoader } from './loaders/api-loader';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: apiLoader({ endpoint: process.env.API_URL }),
});

export const collections = {
  products,
};
```

This configures a live collection using a custom `apiLoader` to fetch data at runtime. The loader must implement the `LiveLoader` interface with `loadCollection()` and `loadEntry()` methods.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#definelivecollection

Set `accept: 'form'` on `defineAction()` and provide a Zod `input` schema; form data is parsed into an object keyed by each input's `name` attribute, then validated by that schema:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
export const server = {
  newsletter: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      email: z.email(),
      terms: z.boolean(),
    }),
    handler: async ({ email, terms }) => { /* ... */ },
  })
}
```

Validation failures are surfaced via `isInputError(error)`, whose `error.fields` object holds messages per input name.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Define build-time collections in `src/content.config.ts` (`.js`/`.mjs` also supported) with `defineCollection()`, giving each a required `loader` and an optional but recommended Zod `schema`, then export a single `collections` object:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { glob, file } from 'astro/loaders';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ base: './src/content/blog', pattern: '**/*.{md,mdx}' }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    description: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    updatedDate: z.coerce.date().optional(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog };
```

Every frontmatter property must be defined with a Zod type; schemas validate entries (violations produce an error) and power generated TypeScript types. Restart the dev server or sync the content layer (`s + enter`) after schema changes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

Yes, the `event.viewTransition` property exists even when the browser doesn't support the View Transitions API. In that case, Astro provides a polyfill object that implements the same API surface as the native `ViewTransition`, but without DOM integration.

You can interact with this object using its methods like `.updateCallbackDone`, `.ready`, and `.finished` promises, which are useful for managing lifecycle events in your custom swap logic or view transition handling. However, since there's no actual DOM integration, calling `.skipTransition()` or other transition-specific APIs won't have any visual effect.

Example:
```js
document.addEventListener("astro:before-swap", (event) => {
  // Access the polyfilled ViewTransition object
  const vt = event.viewTransition;

  // Use its promises for control flow
  vt.ready.then(() => console.log('View transition is ready'));
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/view-transitions/

When the user presses the browser's back button, the `navigationType` attribute in lifecycle events like `astro:before-preparation` reports `"popstate"`.

This differs from a navigation that creates a new history entry (such as clicking a link with `data-astro-history="push"`) which reports `"pushstate"` or `"replacestate"` depending on how the navigation was initiated. The `"popstate"` type indicates that the navigation was triggered by the browser's history stack, not by user interaction with links.

Example:
```ts
window.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', (event) => {
  console.log(event.navigationType); // "popstate" when using back button
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-transitions/#navigationtype

To configure a live collection’s schema for data validation, use `defineLiveCollection()` in `src/live.config.ts` and define a Zod schema under the `schema` property. The schema ensures that data from the live loader conforms to the expected structure and types at runtime.

Example:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: apiLoader({ endpoint: process.env.API_URL }),
  schema: z.object({
    id: z.string(),
    name: z.string(),
    price: z.number(),
    createdAt: z.coerce.date(),
  }),
});
```

This Zod schema validates and transforms data when queried, ensuring type safety and catching validation errors as `AstroError` objects.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#using-zod-schemas-with-live-collections

The `security.allowedDomains` configuration option defines a list of permitted host patterns for incoming requests during on-demand rendering. The `app.getAllowedDomains()` method returns this same list from the App instance.

An adapter needs to call `app.getAllowedDomains()` when it must validate or filter incoming requests based on these allowed domains, particularly in serverless or edge environments where request handling occurs outside of Astro's standard rendering pipeline.

Example:
```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  const allowedDomains = app.getAllowedDomains();
  // Validate request origin against allowed domains
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/#securityalloweddomains

The `astro:before-swap` lifecycle event provides access to the new page’s document via the `newDocument` attribute, allowing you to modify its DOM before it replaces the current page.

Example:
```js
document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', (event) => {
  const elementToRemove = event.newDocument.querySelector('#element-to-remove');
  if (elementToRemove) {
    elementToRemove.remove();
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-transitions/#astrobefore-swap-event

Throw the `ActionError` class from `astro:actions` inside your action's `handler()`, passing a status `code` (e.g. `"UNAUTHORIZED"`, `"NOT_FOUND"`) and an optional `message`. This sets the HTTP status code and surfaces the error on the result's `error` property instead of returning `undefined`:

```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";
import { z } from "astro/zod";
export const server = {
  likePost: defineAction({
    input: z.object({ postId: z.string() }),
    handler: async (input, ctx) => {
      if (!ctx.cookies.has('user-session')) {
        throw new ActionError({
          code: "UNAUTHORIZED",
          message: "User must be logged in.",
        });
      }
    },
  }),
};
```

Callers then check `error?.code === 'UNAUTHORIZED'` to render targeted feedback.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

To define a type-safe environment variable for a feature flag using `envField.boolean()`, set the `context` to `"client"` and `access` to `"public"`, and provide a `default` value of `true`.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      FEATURE_FLAG: envField.boolean({ context: "client", access: "public", default: true })
    }
  }
});
```

This variable will be available in both client and server bundles and can be imported from `astro:env/client`.

`memoryCache()`, imported from `astro/config`, is the built-in in-memory cache provider factory for route caching of SSR responses (added with `cache` in `astro@7.0.0`). It takes optional `MemoryCacheProviderOptions` and returns a `CacheProviderConfig` you pass to the `cache.provider` option:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache(),
  },
  routeRules: {
    '/blog/[...path]': { maxAge: 300, swr: 60 },
  },
});
```

Adapters may also configure a provider automatically; once set, control caching per-request with `Astro.cache.set()` in routes and `context.cache.set()` in middleware or API routes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/

`app.removeBase(pathname)` (type `(pathname: string) => string`, added in `astro@1.6.4`) strips the configured `base` from the given path, which is useful when you need to look up assets from the filesystem. In a custom adapter server entrypoint:

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';
const app = createApp();
export async function handler(event, context) {
  const pathname = app.removeBase(new URL(event.url).pathname);
  // use pathname to look up assets from the filesystem
}
```

Without removing the base, a request like `/docs/logo.png` (with `base: '/docs'`) would resolve to a filesystem path that does not exist.

Create `src/middleware.ts` and export an `onRequest` handler that awaits `next()`, logs the response's `status`, then returns the response:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const response = await next();
  console.log(response.status);
  return response;
});
```

Middleware must return a `Response` object — either directly or by returning the result of calling `next()` — otherwise Astro throws an error.

Import `reference` from `astro:content` and call it inside a collection's Zod schema to declare a property as an entry (or array of entries) from another collection, identified by `id`:
```
import { defineCollection, reference } from "astro:content";
const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ base: "./src/content/blog", pattern: "**/*.{md,mdx}" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    author: reference("authors"),
    relatedPosts: z.array(reference("blog")),
  }),
});
```
Each reference resolves to an object with `collection` and `id` keys, giving type checking, autocomplete, and validation from the referenced collection's schema.

The `actions` object, imported from `astro:actions`, exposes every action you defined in `src/actions/index.ts` so they can be called by name from components, client scripts, and form attributes. From a page script, import it and await the call, which resolves to `{ data, error }`:

```js
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const { data, error } = await actions.likePost({ postId });
if (error?.code === 'UNAUTHORIZED') setShowLogin(true);
else if (error) return;
```

In a form submission script, pass `FormData` directly:

```js
const { error } = await actions.newsletter(new FormData(form));
if (!error) navigate('/confirmation');
```

Alternatively, use it as a zero-JS form target: `<form method="POST" action={actions.logout}>`, which requires the page to be on-demand rendered. On the server, read results with `Astro.getActionResult()`, or use `.orThrow()` to skip error handling.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

When querying a live collection, the Zod schema takes precedence over the loader’s loose typing. The schema defines the final TypeScript type and validation rules for the entry data.

Example:
```ts
const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: apiLoader({ endpoint: process.env.API_URL }),
  schema: z.object({
    price: z.number(),
  }),
});
```

If `price` is a string from the API, Zod will coerce it to a number during validation. If coercion fails, an error is thrown.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/#using-zod-schemas-with-live-collections

Import `StaticPaths` from `astro:static-paths` (added in `astro@6.0.0`) and construct it with an app instance, then call `getAll()` to retrieve every `{ pathname, route: RouteData }` that must be prerendered. This lets a custom prerenderer (set via `setPrerenderer()` in the `astro:build:start` hook) collect paths from within its own runtime, such as `workerd`. Note the module only provides a real implementation in the `prerender` Vite environment; elsewhere it returns a no-op implementation.

```js
import { createApp } from 'astro/app/entrypoint';
import { StaticPaths } from 'astro:static-paths';
const app = createApp();
const staticPaths = new StaticPaths(app);
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Throw an `ActionError` with `code: "BAD_REQUEST"` from your action `handler()`, imported from `astro:actions` alongside `defineAction`:

```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";
import { z } from "astro/zod";

export const server = {
  likePost: defineAction({
    input: z.object({ postId: z.string() }),
    handler: async (input, ctx) => {
      throw new ActionError({ code: "BAD_REQUEST", message: "Invalid post." });
    },
  }),
};
```

The error surfaces on the `error` property of the action result (type `ActionError`, carrying `code` and `message`), so callers check `if (error)` or branch on `error.code`. Validation failures also produce an `ActionError` — specifically an input validation error detectable with `isInputError()`, whose `fields` object holds per-field messages. A separate error, `ActionsReturnedInvalidDataError`, is thrown only when a handler returns non-serializable data.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

After retrieving a `cacheHint` from `getLiveCollection()`, pass it to `Astro.cache.set()` to apply the caching strategy defined by the live loader.

```astro
---import { getLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const { entries, cacheHint } = await getLiveCollection('products');
if (cacheHint) {
  Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
}
---
<ul>
  {entries.map((p) => <li>{p.data.name}</li>)}
</ul>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/#getlivecollection

`middleware()` runs Astro's middleware chain from `src/middleware.ts`, while `pages()` renders the matched route and produces the page's response. `middleware()` accepts a `next` callback invoked at the bottom of the chain to produce that response — typically by calling `pages()`:

```ts
import { FetchState, middleware, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const response = await middleware(state, (s) => pages(s));
    return response;
  },
};
```

Use the combined form whenever `src/middleware.ts` should run; call `pages()` directly only to bypass user middleware. Both handlers set `state.response`, so you can inspect or post-process it afterwards (e.g. `i18n(state, response)`).

The `envField.string()` helper is used to define a typed environment variable with a string data type in Astro's type-safe environment variables schema. It allows you to specify the variable’s context (client or server), access level (public or secret), and additional properties like `optional` or `default`.

To validate a URL-type variable, use `envField.string()` with a custom validation function via the `validate` option in your schema.

Example:

```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      API_URL: envField.string({
        context: "client",
        access: "public",
        validate(value) {
          try {
            new URL(value);
            return true;
          } catch {
            return false;
          }
        },
      }),
    },
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

`envField` is a helper imported from `astro/config` used inside the `env.schema` option of your Astro config to declare type-safe environment variables. Each entry states its data type (`string`, `number`, `enum`, `boolean`), a `context` (`"client"` or `"server"`), an `access` (`"public"` or `"secret"`), and optional extras like `optional` or `default`:

```js
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      API_URL: envField.string({ context: "client", access: "public", optional: true }),
      PORT: envField.number({ context: "server", access: "public", default: 4321 }),
      API_SECRET: envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" }),
    },
  },
});
```

Types are generated during `astro dev` or `astro build` (or `astro sync` alone), and values are imported from `astro:env/client` or `astro:env/server`. `context: "client"` combined with `access: "secret"` is not supported.

`app.getAllowedDomains()` has type `() => Partial<RemotePattern>[] | undefined` (added in `astro@5.14.2`); it returns the permitted host patterns for incoming requests under on-demand rendering, or `undefined` when none are configured.

Configure it via `security.allowedDomains` in `astro.config.mjs` — type `Array<RemotePattern>`, default `[]`:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    allowedDomains: [
      { hostname: '**.example.com', protocol: 'https' },
      { hostname: 'staging.myapp.com', protocol: 'https', port: '443' }
    ]
  }
});
```

Each pattern may specify `protocol`, `hostname`, and `port`, with `*` (one subdomain level) and `**` (any depth) wildcards. When configured, Astro validates the `X-Forwarded-Host` header against these patterns, ignoring mismatched headers to prevent host header injection. Use `[{}]` to allow all domains.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

`i18n(state, response)` post-processes an already-rendered response against your `i18n` configuration, handling locale redirects, 404s for invalid locales, and fallback routing. Call it after rendering:

```ts
import { FetchState, i18n, middleware, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const response = await middleware(state, (s) => pages(s));
    return i18n(state, response);
  },
};
```

Locale behavior is configured in `astro.config.mjs` under `i18n`: `defaultLocale`, `locales`, and `routing` — notably `prefixDefaultLocale` (default `false`, so only non-default languages get a `/[locale]/` prefix), `redirectToDefaultLocale` (redirects `/` to `/[defaultLocale]` when prefixed), `fallbackType`, or `routing: "manual"`. Since 4.6.0, `"manual"` disables Astro's i18n middleware and forbids the other `routing` options; you own routing logic but can still invoke Astro's i18n handling yourself — precisely where the fetch-pipeline `i18n()` call is most useful. Common scenarios: redirecting un-prefixed default-locale URLs, rejecting invalid locale segments with 404s, and applying locale fallbacks.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Construct `StaticPaths` from an app instance inside the handler and await `getAll()`, which resolves to `Array<{ pathname: string, route: RouteData }>`:

```js
import { StaticPaths } from 'astro:static-paths';
export function createHandler(app) {
  return async (request) => {
    const { pathname } = new URL(request.url);
    if (pathname === '/__astro_static_paths') {
      const staticPaths = new StaticPaths(app);
      const paths = await staticPaths.getAll();
      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ paths }));
    }
    // ... handle other requests
  };
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-static-paths/

Disable streaming per framework integration: `@astrojs/react` accepts the experimental `experimentalDisableStreaming` option:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import react from '@astrojs/react';
export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [react({ experimentalDisableStreaming: true })]
});
```

It's mainly for libraries that don't work well with streaming, such as some CSS-in-JS solutions. Note that Astro's SSR streams HTML by default, sending each component as it's ready — that's what lets the `<head>`, `<body>`, and early markup reach the browser while other components still fetch data. Without streaming you lose that progressive rendering, so keep data fetches out of a page's frontmatter (put them in smaller components), otherwise the browser waits for every fetch before receiving any HTML.

To define a middleware function in Astro 7 using TypeScript, import `defineMiddleware` from `"astro:middleware"` and use it to wrap your middleware logic. The `context` and `next` parameters are automatically typed.

src/middleware.ts

```
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  // Your middleware logic here
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/middleware/

The `values` property in `envField.enum()` defines the allowed string values for the environment variable. It is required and must be an array of strings.

If omitted, Astro throws an unknown configuration error because the schema validation cannot determine the valid options for the enum field.

Example:
```ts
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      DEBUG_MODE: envField.enum({
        context: "server",
        access: "public",
        values: ['info', 'warnings', 'errors'], // required
      }),
    }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-config/#envfieldenum

`CollectionEntry<'name'>` is a utility type from `astro:content` that types an entry according to its collection schema: it takes the collection name as a string argument and inherits all properties of that schema, giving autocomplete and type-checking. Use it to type component props or variables, then query with `getCollection()` and filter on frontmatter via the `data` object:

```astro
---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const draftBlogPosts = await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => {
  return data.draft === true;
});
---
```

Each entry exposes a unique `id`, a `data` object with all defined properties, and a `body` for Markdown/MDX/Markdoc documents.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/

By default, `app.match()` does not match prerendered routes, even if they exist. To include prerendered routes in matching, pass `true` as the second argument to `app.match(request, true)`.

Example:
```ts
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  if (app.match(request, true)) {
    const response = await app.render(request);
    return response;
  } else {
    return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
  }
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-app/#appmatch

Define actions with `defineAction()` from `astro:actions` in the exported `server` object; set `accept: 'form'` and an `input` Zod schema (from `astro/zod`) to validate the submitted data:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
export const server = {
  newsletter: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      email: z.email(),
      terms: z.boolean(),
    }),
    handler: async ({ email, terms }) => { /* ... */ },
  })
}
```

Form data is parsed into an object keyed by each input's `name`; omit `input` to receive the raw `FormData` instead. Use `enctype="multipart/form-data"` for file uploads.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-actions/

`getLiveCollection()` accepts an optional `filter` object whose shape is defined by the collection's loader, letting you query only some items in a live collection at request time. Omitting the filter returns all entries; passing criteria narrows them per your API's capabilities.

```astro
---
import { getLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
const { entries: allProducts } = await getLiveCollection('products');
const { entries: featuredProducts } = await getLiveCollection('products', { featured: true });
---
```

Unlike build-time `getCollection()`, which takes a callback filtering on `id`/`data`, live filtering happens server-side through the loader. Requires an adapter for on-demand rendering.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/

The `pages` handler (added in `astro@7.0.0`) wraps Astro's page rendering as a Hono middleware function, so a Hono app exported from `src/fetch.ts` can serve your Astro pages. Register it with `app.use()`, typically together with the other Astro handlers:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { actions, middleware, pages, i18n } from 'astro/hono';
const app = new Hono();
app.use(logger());
app.use(actions());
app.use(middleware());
app.use(pages());
app.use(i18n());
export default app;
```

Because it is ordinary Hono middleware, use it effectively by ordering it after other middleware — a logger, or an auth guard such as `app.use('/dashboard/*', requireAuth)` — so protected routes are checked before Astro renders them.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-hono/

Use `trailingSlash()` from `astro/fetch` in your custom fetch pipeline to automatically normalize URLs. It checks if the request pathname needs trailing-slash normalization per your config and returns a redirect `Response` when needed, otherwise `undefined`. Call it early in the pipeline before rendering.

```ts
import { FetchState, trailingSlash, pages } from "astro/fetch";
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const state = new FetchState(request);
    const redirect = trailingSlash(state);
    if (redirect) return redirect;
    return pages(state);
  },
};
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-fetch/

Add an `i18n` object to `defineConfig()` in `astro.config.mjs` (added in `astro@3.5.0`), with required `locales` and `defaultLocale` fields:

```js
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "fr"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
    routing: { prefixDefaultLocale: true },
  },
});
```

`locales` accepts plain codes or objects mapping `codes` to a custom URL `path` (e.g. `{ path: "french", codes: ["fr", "fr-BR", "fr-CA"] }`); folder names must match the `locales` items (or the `path`). Optional `fallback` maps missing translations to another locale. `astro.config.js`, `.mjs`, and `.ts` are all supported; `.mjs` is recommended.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

`LocalImageService` defines the hooks a local image transformation service must provide: `getURL()`, `parseURL()`, and `transform()`, with optional hooks such as `getHTMLAttributes()` and `getSrcSet()` also supported. `getURL()` returns the endpoint URL that generates the image (used in dev and on-demand rendering, unused at build), `parseURL()` parses that URL back into transform options (local services only), and `transform()` receives the input buffer and must return `{ data, format }` so the correct MIME type is served. Since Astro v7.3.0, hooks also receive a logger. You can extend the built-in service:

```ts
import { baseService } from "astro/assets";
const newImageService = {
  getURL: baseService.getURL,
  parseURL: baseService.parseURL,
  getHTMLAttributes: baseService.getHTMLAttributes,
  async transform(inputBuffer, transformOptions) { /* ... */ },
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/image-service-reference/

Call `setActionResult()` from the `getActionContext()` utility (imported from `astro:actions`) inside your middleware, passing the action name and the result **serialized with `serializeActionResult()`** — serialization is required to handle non-JSON return values (`Map`, `Date`) and the `ActionError` object.

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from 'astro:middleware';
import { getActionContext } from 'astro:actions';
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const { action, setActionResult, serializeActionResult } = getActionContext(context);
  if (action?.calledFrom === 'form') {
    const result = await action.handler();
    setActionResult(action.name, serializeActionResult(result));
  }
  return next();
});
```

The stored value is what `Astro.getActionResult()` returns on the subsequent page render, enabling persistence across a POST/Redirect/GET cycle.

The `loader` property in `defineCollection()` is for build-time collections, where data is fetched and processed during the build phase. The `loader` property in `defineLiveCollection()` is for live collections, where data is fetched at runtime.

For example, a build-time collection might use the `file()` loader to read local JSON files:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { file } from 'astro/loaders';

const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file('src/data/dogs.json'),
});

export const collections = { dogs };
```

A live collection uses a custom `loader` function that fetches data at runtime:

```ts
import { defineLiveCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { storeLoader } from '@example/astro-loader';

const products = defineLiveCollection({
  loader: storeLoader({ apiKey: process.env.STORE_API_KEY }),
});

export const collections = { products };
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-content/

Import `srcDir` from `astro:config/server` and resolve paths against it inside your integration hooks; it is a string (default `"./src"`, absolute or relative to the project root):

```js
import { srcDir } from "astro:config/server";
import { writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";

export default function() {
  return {
    name: "internal-integration",
    hooks: {
      "astro:build:done": () => {
        let file = new URL("result.json", srcDir);
        let data = JSON.stringify([]);
        writeFileSync(fileURLToPath(file), data, "utf-8");
      },
    },
  };
}
```

The `astro:config` virtual module (added in `astro@5.7.0`) exposes every config value from `/server`, while `/client` only exposes values safe for the browser — use `/server` for filesystem paths in config files and integration code.

`notFound()` returns a 404 `Response` for use in your i18n routing middleware when `i18n.routing` is set to `"manual"` (added in `astro@4.6.0`, type `(context: APIContext, response?: Response) => Promise<Response> | undefined`). It triggers when the current path isn't a root (`/` or `/<base>`) and the URL doesn't contain a locale; if you pass a `Response`, the emitted response keeps the original's headers. Return it instead of calling `next()`:

```
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { notFound } from "astro:i18n";
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  const pathNotFound = notFound(context);
  if (pathNotFound) {
    return pathNotFound;
  }
  return next();
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-i18n/

Add the `env.schema` option to your Astro config and register each variable with the `envField` helper, specifying `context` (`"client"` or `"server"`), `access` (`"secret"` or `"public"`), and optional properties like `optional` or `default`:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, envField } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      API_URL: envField.string({ context: "client", access: "public", optional: true }),
      PORT: envField.number({ context: "server", access: "public", default: 4321 }),
      API_SECRET: envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" }),
    }
  }
})
```

Variables are typed as string, number, enum, or boolean, then imported from `astro:env/client` or `astro:env/server`. Types are generated by `astro dev`/`astro build`, or run `astro sync` to generate them only. Secret client variables (`context: "client"` + `access: "secret"`) are not supported.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

`app.match(request, allowPrerenderedRoutes = false)` determines whether a request is matched by the Astro app's routing rules, returning `RouteData | undefined`. Use it when you want to handle 404s yourself instead of relying on a `404.astro` file (which `app.render()` will handle automatically):

```js
import { createApp } from "astro/app/entrypoint";
const app = createApp();
export const handle = async (request: Request) => {
  if (app.match(request)) {
    return await app.render(request);
  } else {
    return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
  }
};
```

By default prerendered routes aren't returned even when matched; pass `true` as the second argument to include them.

Edit or create `src/env.d.ts` and declare `ImportMetaEnv` with `XATA_API_KEY` as a required string and `XATA_BRANCH` as optional, then extend `ImportMeta` so `import.meta.env` is typed:

```ts
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly XATA_API_KEY: string;
  readonly XATA_BRANCH?: string;
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

This gives IntelliSense and type safety for both variables wherever you use `import.meta.env` in the project.

`.env` provides the actual value at runtime — Astro loads `NEON_DATABASE_URL` from the project-root `.env` file into `import.meta.env` — while the declarations in `src/env.d.ts` only improve TypeScript support by typing the variable:

```
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly NEON_DATABASE_URL: string;
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

So `.env` supplies the connection string (with `.env.development`/`.env.production` for branching); `src/env.d.ts` merely makes `import.meta.env.NEON_DATABASE_URL` type-check.

