Create `src/pages/api/auth/register.ts` with a POST handler that accepts form data (email/password), validates input, calls `supabase.auth.signUp()`, and redirects on success. For static output, add `export const prerender = false`.

```ts
// With `output: 'static'` configured:
// export const prerender = false;
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { supabase } from "../../../lib/supabase";

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request, redirect }) => {
  const formData = await request.formData();
  const email = formData.get("email")?.toString();
  const password = formData.get("password")?.toString();

  if (!email || !password) {
    return new Response("Email and password are required", { status: 400 });
  }

  const { error } = await supabase.auth.signUp({
    email,
    password,
  });

  if (error) {
    return new Response(error.message, { status: 500 });
  }

  return redirect("/signin");
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/supabase/

Because any environment variable with the `PUBLIC_` prefix is intentionally exposed to the browser bundle — Astro inlines it for client-side code, so your database URL and auth token shipped to every visitor. Remove the prefix so they stay server-only: name them `TURSO_DATABASE_URL` and `TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN` in `.env`, and read them only from server-side code, e.g. in `src/turso.ts`:

```ts
import { createClient } from "@libsql/client/web";
export const turso = createClient({
  url: import.meta.env.TURSO_DATABASE_URL,
  authToken: import.meta.env.TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN,
});
```

Then rotate the leaked token, since it has already been public.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/turso/

Instantiate and export the Prisma Client with the `PrismaPg` adapter in a shared file like `src/lib/prisma.ts`. This ensures the same client instance is reused across your Astro app.

The file should look like this:

```ts
// src/lib/prisma.ts
import { PrismaPg } from '@prisma/adapter-pg';
import { PrismaClient } from '../../prisma/generated/client';

const connectionString = import.meta.env.DATABASE_URL;
const adapter = new PrismaPg({ connectionString });
const prisma = new PrismaClient({ adapter });

export default prisma;
```

This client is safe for use in both SSR pages and API routes.

Type the layout with the `MarkdownLayoutProps` helper, giving your frontmatter shape as its type parameter; `frontmatter`, `url`, `headings`, and the rest of the layout props then become type-safe:

```astro
---
import type { MarkdownLayoutProps } from 'astro';
type Props = MarkdownLayoutProps<{
  title: string;
  author: string;
  date: string;
}>;
const { frontmatter, url } = Astro.props;
---
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(url, Astro.site).pathname}>
  <title>{frontmatter.title}</title>
</head>
```

Building the canonical link: pass the layout `url` (e.g. `/en/guides/markdown-content`) as the path and your configured `Astro.site` as the base, then take `.pathname`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/basics/layouts/

Add a TypeScript `Props` interface in the layout's frontmatter, listing every prop with its type and no optional markers:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  publishDate: string;
  viewCount: number;
}
const { title, description, publishDate, viewCount } = Astro.props;
---
```

The Astro VS Code Extension automatically looks for the `Props` interface, giving you type safety and autocompletion both inside the component and wherever it is used (an `export` statement is allowed but not required). Declaring the type changes nothing about how the consuming page passes the props — the syntax stays identical; a page that forgets `viewCount` is now flagged by the type checker/editor instead of silently rendering.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/typescript/

Treat the `error` prop as `unknown` and narrow it before reading any property, falling back to generic content otherwise — this both prevents the blow-up and avoids disclosure:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  error: unknown;
}
const { error } = Astro.props;
---
<div>{error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Unknown error"}</div>
```

Do not display the stack; evaluate the error first and render only appropriate, non-sensitive content based on what was thrown.

Use `getArticles()`, imported from `../../api/drupal` (the file `src/api/drupal.ts`) in `src/pages/articles/index.astro`; it returns a typed array of entries as `Promise<DrupalNode[]>`, with `DrupalNode` imported from `../../types`.

