Fetch the Payload REST route (`/api/posts` by default), then map over the `posts.docs` array and render each `title` and `content` with `set:html` in `src/pages/index.astro`:

```astro
---
import HomeLayout from "../layouts/HomeLayout.astro";
const res = await fetch("http://localhost:5000/api/posts");
const posts = await res.json();
---
<HomeLayout title='Astro Blog'>
  {
    posts.docs.map((post) => (
      <h2 set:html={post.title} />
      <p set:html={post.content} />
    ))
  }
</HomeLayout>
```

Payload wraps the documents in `docs`; top-level fields are pagination metadata.

Create `src/env.d.ts` and extend the global `ImportMetaEnv` interface (Astro already provides a base type for `import.meta.env` in `astro/client.d.ts`), then declare `HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT` on it:

```ts
// src/env.d.ts
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT: string;
  // more env variables...
}
interface ImportMeta {
  readonly env: ImportMetaEnv;
}
```

The file must be located in `src/` (i.e. `src/env.d.ts`) and extends the global types, giving you TypeScript IntelliSense when reading `import.meta.env.HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT`. Without `PUBLIC_`-prefixed names, the variable remains server-side only. Alternatively, register it in the `env.schema` with `envField` for generated type-safe imports from `astro:env/server`.

Export a `TextProps` interface for the field, a `Props` interface wrapping it, destructure from `Astro.props`, and inject the markup with `set:html`:

```astro
---
export interface TextProps {
  text: string;
}
export interface Props {
  item: TextProps;
}
const { item } = Astro.props;
---
<div set:html={item.text} />
```

When the field is not a simple string — a modular content field — it isn't handled by this component: query each record type with inline fragments (`... on TextRecord { text(markdown: true) }`, `... on ImageRecord { image { url } }`), then map each item in the template with a `switch` on `item._modelApiKey`, returning the matching block component (`<Text item={item} />`, `<Image item={item} />`) or `null` in the default case.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/cms/datocms/

A 200 only confirms the HTTP request succeeded; DatoCMS reports GraphQL failures in the response body, and `home` is absent/`null` when the query can't resolve it. Likely causes: an invalid or missing `DATOCMS_API_KEY`, no "Home" model created with the "Single Instance" toggle, or its content never saved — the guide's `const data = json.data.home` then yields `undefined`.

Validate before dereferencing:

```astro
---
const json = await response.json();
if (json.errors || !json.data?.home) {
  console.error(json.errors ?? 'No home record');
}
const data = json.data?.home;
---
<h1>{data?.title}</h1>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/cms/datocms/

To generate static paths for individual blog posts in Astro 7 using a dynamic route at `src/pages/blog/[slug].astro`, you must export a `getStaticPaths()` function that returns an array of objects with `params` and optional `props`. This function fetches blog post data, maps each item to a path, and passes relevant props to the page component.

Example implementation:

```astro
---
import { cosmicClient } from "../../lib/cosmic";
import type { BlogPost } from "../../lib/cosmic";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const entries = await cosmicClient.getEntries<BlogPost>({
    bucket: "blog-posts",
    type: "posts"
  });

  return entries.map((item) => ({
    params: { slug: item.slug },
    props: {
      title: item.title,
      content: item.content,
      date: new Date(item.date).toLocaleDateString(),
    }
  }));
}

const { content, title, date } = Astro.props;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title>{title}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <time>{date}</time>
    <article set:html={content} />
  </body>
</html>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/#getstaticpaths

Use `getEmDashCollection()`, because it queries an entire collection and returns multiple entries — what a list of posts needs — while `getEmDashEntry()` fetches a single entry by slug and belongs on the individual post page.

```astro
---
import { getEmDashCollection } from "emdash";
const { entries: posts } = await getEmDashCollection("posts", {
  status: "published",
});
---
```

Both follow the live collections pattern and return results at request time, so published changes appear without a rebuild.

---
import type { Blogpost } from "flotiq-api-ts";
import { flotiq } from "../../lib/flotiq";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await flotiq.BlogpostAPI.list();
  return posts.data?.map((post) => ({
    params: { slug: post.slug },
    props: post
  })) || [];
}

const post: Blogpost = Astro.props;
---
<html lang="en">
  <title>{post.title}</title>
  <body>
    <h1>{post.title}</h1>
    <div set:html={post.content}/>
  </body>
</html>

Import `loadEnv` from `vite` and call `const env = loadEnv("", process.cwd(), 'APOS');`, then use `JSON.stringify` on each value inside `vite.define`:
```
vite: {
  define: {
    'process.env.APOS_EXTERNAL_FRONT_KEY': JSON.stringify(env.APOS_EXTERNAL_FRONT_KEY),
    'process.env.APOS_HOST': JSON.stringify(env.APOS_HOST)
  }
}
```
`JSON.stringify` is required because `define` performs literal code substitution, so the value must be a quoted string literal.

Minimal static-site `wrangler.jsonc`:

```json
{
  "name": "my-astro-app",
  "compatibility_date": "YYYY-MM-DD", // Update to the day you deploy
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist"
  }
}
```

Preview locally with `npx astro build && npx wrangler dev`, then deploy with `npx astro build && npx wrangler deploy`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/deploy/cloudflare/

Set these three fields in `wrangler.jsonc`:

```json
{
  "main": "@astrojs/cloudflare/entrypoints/server",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "assets": { "directory": "./dist", "binding": "ASSETS" }
}
```

`nodejs_compat` is required because Cloudflare Workers are not a Node.js runtime: the flag enables Cloudflare's support for most Node.js runtime APIs (`node:buffer`, `node:crypto`, `node:path`, and many others) so server-side code that imports them can build and run. Wrangler can also inject polyfills for Node.js APIs not yet in the runtime, which requires `nodejs_compat` plus a compatibility date of 2024-09-23 or later. (For simple projects this file is optional — Astro generates a default configuration.)

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/

The error occurs because `getImage()` is a server-only function and cannot be called on the client. To use an optimized image in client-side code, call `getImage()` during server rendering (e.g., in component frontmatter), then pass the resulting `src` to the client.

Example:
```astro
---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import myImage from '../assets/my_image.png';

const optimizedImage = await getImage({ src: myImage, width: 300 });
---

<script>
  const imageSrc = {{ optimizedImage.src }};
  // Use imageSrc in client-side code
  document.getElementById('myImage').src = imageSrc;
</script>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-image-not-used-on-server/

In Astro middleware, calling `next()` as a plain statement (e.g., `next()`) will execute the next middleware in the chain but does not return the response to the caller. Returning `next()` (e.g., `return next()`) passes the response back up the chain and ensures that the middleware pipeline continues correctly.

Example:
```js
// ❌ Incorrect: Does not return a Response
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  next(); // This does not propagate the response
});

// ✅ Correct: Returns the Response from next()
export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  return next(); // Propagates the response properly
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/modules/astro-middleware/#middlewarehandler

Your `getStaticPaths()` function returned an array containing objects missing the required `params` property, or the `params` property was empty/undefined. Each object must have a `params` key with an object value specifying route parameters.

Example of invalid code:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: {} }, // ❌ Empty params
    { } // ❌ Missing params entirely
  ];
}
```

Correct usage:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }, // ✅ Correct
  ];
}
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-expected-params/

Return a `Response` instance from the page — only `Response` objects can be returned from Astro files:

```astro
---
return new Response(null, { status: 404, statusText: 'Not found'});
// Alternatively, for redirects, `Astro.redirect()` also returns an instance of `Response`
return Astro.redirect('/login');
---
```

For a custom 404 page on a prerendered site, create `404.astro` or `404.md` in `src/pages`; it builds to a `404.html` that most deploy services use.

Install `@astrojs/vue` and call `vue()` from the `integrations` array in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import vue from '@astrojs/vue';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [vue()],
});
```

The fastest setup is `npx astro add vue`, which installs the package and updates the config for you. Manual install: `npm install @astrojs/vue`, plus `npm install vue` if you see a `Cannot find package 'vue'` warning at startup. Options such as `appEntrypoint`, `jsx: true`, `devtools: true`, or `@vitejs/plugin-vue` template compiler options are passed directly to the `vue({...})` call.

