All actions must be exported from the `server` object in `src/actions/index.ts`; that file is what the framework resolves action names against, so organization affects resolution directly. You may define actions inline, or move them to separate files and import them — including grouping into nested objects:

```ts
// src/actions/user.ts
export const user = {
  getUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
  createUser: defineAction(/* ... */),
}

// src/actions/index.ts
import { user } from './user';
export const server = {
  myAction: defineAction({ /* ... */ }),
  user,
}
```

They're then callable as `actions.user.getUser()` and `actions.user.createUser()`. If an action isn't re-exported through `server` (or the name doesn't match after a rename), you get `ActionNotFoundError`. Actions also require server output: add an adapter and enable on-demand rendering, or you'll hit `ActionsWithoutServerOutputError`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Astro rejects your middleware because it doesn't return a `Response` or call `next()`. Even though your middleware executes and modifies `context.locals`, it must explicitly return a response or invoke the `next()` function to continue the request lifecycle.

Example:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware((context, next) => {
  context.locals.someData = false;
  return next(); // Required to proceed
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/middleware-no-data-or-next-called/

Astro requires endpoints to explicitly return a `Response` or a `Promise` resolving to a `Response`. If an async handler's helper function doesn’t return a value, the promise resolves to `undefined`, which is not a valid response and triggers `EndpointDidNotReturnAResponse`.

Even though your handler is typed as returning `Promise<Response>`, if the helper function does not explicitly return a `Response`, the resolved value becomes `undefined`. You must ensure that all code paths in your async functions return a proper `Response` object.

Example:
```ts
import type { APIContext } from 'astro';

export async function GET({ request }: APIContext): Promise<Response> {
  const result = await someHelper(); // Ensure this helper always returns Response
  if (!result) {
    return new Response('Not found', { status: 404 });
  }
  return result;
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/endpoint-did-not-return-aresponse/

A "date" must be a valid date error occurs when the `date` field in a content entry does not match the expected format or type defined in the collection schema, even if it appears populated. This typically happens when:

- The value is not a string that can be parsed as a date (e.g., `"2024-13-01"` instead of `"2024-12-01"`)
- The field is present but set to `null`, `undefined`, or an invalid type like a number
- The schema expects a specific format, such as ISO 8601 (`YYYY-MM-DD`), and the entry uses a different one

Example:
```ts
// In src/content.config.ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
const blog = defineCollection({
  schema: ({ image }) =>
    z.object({
      title: z.string(),
      date: z.date(), // expects JavaScript Date object or valid ISO string
    }),
});
```

If an entry provides `date: "invalid-date"` or `date: null`, this error will occur.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/content-entry-data-error/

Astro requires `getStaticPaths()` route parameters to be strings because they are encoded into URLs and must conform to URL path segments.

In your `/route/[id].astro` dynamic route, returning `{ params: { id: 2 } }` fails because the value for `id` is a number (`2`) instead of a string (`'2'`). Even though numbers can be converted to strings in JavaScript, Astro strictly enforces string types for route parameters.

To fix this error, convert numeric IDs to strings:

```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: '1' } }, // Works
    { params: { id: '2' } }, // Works
  ];
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/errors/get-static-paths-invalid-route-param/

For a secure configuration in Astro 7.3.5, use both together as complementary allowlists: `image.domains` for exact hostnames (no wildcards), and `image.remotePatterns` for flexible matching with protocol/hostname/pathname wildcards. Astro only optimizes remote images whose source URL matches at least one allowed entry.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['cdn.example.com'],
    remotePatterns: [
      {
        protocol: 'https',
        hostname: '**.amazonaws.com',
        pathname: '/images/**',
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

This ensures you allow only the specific sources you intend while keeping the allowlist explicit and auditable.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

The error occurs because Astro’s static site generation mode requires a `getStaticPaths()` function in dynamic routes to determine which paths to prerender at build time. Your file `src/pages/blog/[slug].astro` is a dynamic route, but it's missing the required export.

To fix this, add an export for `getStaticPaths()` that returns an array of objects with `params` matching your route parameter (`slug`). For example:

```ts
// src/pages/blog/[slug].astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { slug: 'first-post' } },
    { params: { slug: 'second-post' } },
