The main difference is that `injectScript('head-inline')` injects script content directly into a `<script>` tag inside the `<head>`, while `injectScript('page')` handles the script via Vite's bundling process, including optimizations and module resolution.

Use `'head-inline'` for simple scripts that don't need processing or optimization. Use `'page'` when you want your script to be bundled with other page scripts, benefiting from Vite's optimizations and import resolution.

Example:
```ts
// head-inline: injected as-is into <head>
injectScript('head-inline', 'console.log("inline");');

// page: processed by Vite, optimized and bundled
injectScript('page', 'import { foo } from "./utils.js"; console.log(foo);');
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/integrations-reference/

Yes, you can use multiple experimental features simultaneously in Astro 7 projects by configuring them within the `experimental` property of your `astro.config.mjs` file.

For example, to enable both `collectionStorage` and other experimental flags like `serverIslands`, you would define them together:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';

export default defineConfig({
  experimental: {
    collectionStorage: 'chunked',
    serverIslands: true,
  },
});
```

This approach allows you to combine different experimental features as needed. Note that some flags may have been removed or stabilized in newer versions of Astro, so always check the documentation for compatibility.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/collection-storage/

