});
```

Use `astro:config:setup` only for `addDevToolbarApp()`; all toolbar messaging helpers (`send`, `on`, `onAppInitialized`, `onAppToggled`) live on the `toolbar` object in `astro:server:setup`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

Call `parseData()` from the `LoaderContext` passed to `load()` before `store.set()`. It validates and parses the data according to the collection schema, and returns a `Promise<TData>` resolving to the validated, parsed data object you then write to the store:

```ts
load: async ({ store, logger, parseData }) => {
  const feed = loadFeed(feedUrl);
  store.clear();
  for (const item of feed.items) {
    const id = item.guid;
    const data = await parseData({ id, data: item });
    store.set({ id, data });
  }
},
```

It is the loader's responsibility to do this: no validation happens when getting or setting data in the store.

Specify the version using the `version` family option in the font's `options` object. The default is `'latest'` and it only applies to CDN resolution.
```ts
{
  provider: fontProviders.npm(),
  options: { version: '5.0.0' }
}
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

Use `loadRenderers()` from the `astro:container` virtual module whenever you're running inside Vite — e.g. Vitest tests or Astro integrations — since the virtual module's helpers are only available there; it resolves `getContainerRenderer()` outputs into the `AddServerRenderer[]` you pass as `renderers` to `experimental_AstroContainer.create()`. Outside Vite — on-demand rendered apps or cases where the container runs at runtime inside another "shell" (PHP, Ruby, Java) — the virtual module isn't available, so you must import server/client renderers manually and register them with `addServerRenderer()`/`addClientRenderer()` instead. For static apps and container calls that never run at runtime (e.g. `vitest`), renderers can be added through the Container API automatically.

```js
import { loadRenderers } from "astro:container";
const renderers = await loadRenderers([reactContainerRenderer(), svelteContainerRenderer()]);
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create({ renderers });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/container-reference/

HTML attributes in Astro are converted to strings, so functions can't be passed to HTML elements — your `onClick={handleClick}` never registers a listener, and the button does nothing. Use a client-side `<script>` tag instead and attach the handler in vanilla JavaScript:

```astro
---
---
<button id="button">Click Me</button>
<script>
  function handleClick () {
    console.log("button clicked!");
  }
  document.getElementById("button")?.addEventListener("click", handleClick);
</script>
```

Astro components render to HTML with no client runtime, so interactivity must come from a plain script (or a hydrated framework component).

`ActionCalledFromServerError` is thrown when you invoke an action directly from a server-rendered page or endpoint without wrapping it in `Astro.callAction()`—Astro requires this wrapper in server code so the call returns the standard `{ data, error }` result shape instead of behaving like a client invocation. Use `context.callAction()` inside a server endpoint.

```
---
import { actions } from 'astro:actions';
const searchQuery = Astro.url.searchParams.get('search');
if (searchQuery) {
  const { data, error } = await Astro.callAction(actions.findProduct, { query: searchQuery });
  // handle result
}
---
```

The first argument is the action, the second its input; you get the same `data`/`error` objects as on the client. Alternatively, trigger actions via an HTML form (`action={actions.yourAction}`) and read results with `Astro.getActionResult()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Use `glob()` for the 300 separate files and `file()` for the single array:

```ts
const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.json", base: "./src/data/authors" }),
});
const products = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/products.json"),
});
```

`glob()` fetches entries from directories of separate Markdown/MDX/Markdoc/JSON/YAML/TOML files and auto-generates a unique `id` from each file name; `file()` fetches multiple entries from a single local file, parsing a JSON/YAML array of objects or a TOML file's top-level tables. They don't swap because their unit of input differs: `file()` reads exactly one file and will not auto-generate IDs, so every object must already carry a unique `id`; `glob()` reads one entry per file and derives IDs from file names, so a lone multi-entry array isn't its shape.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Use the `astro:route:setup` hook (added in `astro@4.14.0`), which runs before bundling in `astro build` and on route changes in `astro dev`. It receives `route`, whose `component` identifies the entrypoint and whose `prerender` value you can assign to toggle on-demand rendering per route:

```js
function setPrerender() {
  return {
    name: 'set-prerender',
    hooks: {
      'astro:route:setup': ({ route }) => {
        if (route.component.endsWith('/blog/[slug].astro')) {
          route.prerender = true;
        }
      },
    },
  };
}
```

If left `undefined`, the default follows the `output` option (prerendered for `static`, on-demand for `server`). Note: since v5, dynamic `export const prerender` values in route files are removed — set them via this hook instead.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/integrations-reference/

The `astro:build:done` hook runs after a production build (SSG or SSR) completes and exposes `pages`, `dir`, `assets`, and `logger` for post-processing the output — for example, writing a metadata file into the build directory:

```js
import { writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

hooks: {
  'astro:build:done': async ({ dir }) => {
    const outFile = fileURLToPath(new URL('./my-integration.json', dir));
    await writeFile(outFile, JSON.stringify(metadata));
  }
}
```

`assets` is a `Map<string, URL[]>` of output files grouped by route `pattern` (added in `astro@5.0.0`). Since Astro 6, the `routes` array on this hook is removed — collect routes in `astro:routes:resolved` and look up `distURL` from `assets`. For transforming generated assets, prefer the Vite Plugin API via `astro:config:setup`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/integrations-reference/

No, calling `paginate()` from `getStaticPaths()` is not necessary for a collection of 8 items that fits on a single page. You can directly return the paths without pagination.

Example:
```ts
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection('blog');
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { slug: post.id },
    props: { post }
  }));
}
```

This approach generates one page per item, avoiding unnecessary complexity. Use `paginate()` only when you need to split data across multiple pages.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Have `resolveFont()` return an array of `FontFaceData` objects — its type is `(options: ResolveFontOptions) => Awaitable<{ fonts: FontFaceData[] } | undefined>` — one entry per font file/variant, and the provider is responsible for populating that array:

```ts
resolveFont: ({ familyName, ...rest }) => {
  const fonts = data[familyName];
  if (fonts) {
    return { fonts };
  }
  return undefined;
},
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

`max` controls the maximum number of entries kept in the in-memory cache (type `number`, default `1000`). When the cache exceeds this limit, the least recently used entry is evicted—making the provider an LRU cache. For a site with thousands of pages, size `max` to your working set of hot pages; entries beyond the limit aren't rejected, they simply cycle out as newer ones arrive:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({ max: 500 }),
  },
});
```

Because this provider stores entries in-process, it's suitable only for single-instance deployments.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Configure the Redis driver by importing `sessionDrivers` from `astro/config` and passing `process.env.REDIS_URL` to `sessionDrivers.redis()` in your Astro config. The environment variable must be set in your deployment environment (`.env` locally, platform secrets in production).

```ts
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL })
  }
})
```

Note: `.env` files are not loaded inside configuration files by default; ensure `REDIS_URL` is available in the process environment when Astro config runs.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

Set the top-level `cacheDir` option in `astro.config.mjs`. It defaults to `"./node_modules/.astro"`, and accepts either an absolute path or a path relative to the project root:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cacheDir: './.astro-cache',
});
```

Files in this directory are reused by subsequent builds to speed up build time.

Set `query.exclude: []` in the `memoryCache()` options to include all query parameters in the cache key. By default, tracking parameters like `utm_*` are excluded.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({
      query: { exclude: [] }
    })
  }
});
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Create two files: a config file exporting a function returning `SessionDriverConfig` with `entrypoint` pointing to the runtime file, and a runtime file exporting a default function that returns a `SessionDriver` object with `setItem`, `getItem`, `removeItem` methods using an LRU cache.

driver/config.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriverConfig } from 'astro'
export interface Config { max?: number }
export function memoryDriver(config: Config = {}): SessionDriverConfig {
  return { entrypoint: new URL('./runtime.js', import.meta.url), config }
}
```

driver/runtime.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriver } from 'astro'
import type { Config } from './config'
import { LRUCache } from 'lru-cache'
export default function(config: Config): SessionDriver {
  const cache = new LRUCache({ max: config.max })
  return {
    setItem: async (key, value) => { cache.set(key, value) },
    getItem: async (key) => { return cache.get(key) },
    removeItem: async (key) => { cache.delete(key) },
  }
}
```

Register in `astro.config.ts`:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { memoryDriver } from './driver/config'
export default defineConfig({ session: { driver: memoryDriver({ max: 500 }) } })
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

