Use the logger's `.fork()` method to create a new logger with a custom label. The forked logger prepends the new label instead of the integration's default name.

```ts
const buildLogger = logger.fork("astro-format/build");
buildLogger.info("Build finished.");
```
Output: `[astro-format/build] Build finished.`

If `REDIS_URL` is not set, the Redis driver will fail to connect at runtime, likely throwing a connection error (e.g., `ECONNREFUSED` or authentication failure). Resolve by ensuring the environment variable is defined in your deployment environment (`.env` file locally, platform secrets in production). Use Astro's `env.schema` with `envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" })` to validate it exists at build/dev time.

```ts
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, envField } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  env: {
    schema: {
      REDIS_URL: envField.string({ context: "server", access: "secret" })
    }
  }
})
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

Set `query.exclude` to an empty array `[]`. This overrides the default tracking-parameter exclusion list and includes every query parameter in the cache key.

```js
memoryCache({
  query: { exclude: [] },
});
```

Note that `exclude` and `include` are mutually exclusive — using both together throws an error.

Pass the prop via the `props` option of `renderToString()`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import Card from "../src/components/Card.astro";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  props: { name: "Hello, world!" },
});
```

The `props` option is typed `Record<string, unknown>` and supplies `Astro.props`, which your component destructures as `const { name } = Astro.props`. Then assert `result` contains "Hello, world!".

Set `Astro.response.status` and `Astro.response.statusText` in the page's frontmatter before rendering:

```astro
---
export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode
import { getPost } from '../api';
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) {
  Astro.response.status = 404;
  Astro.response.statusText = 'Not found';
}
---
```

Alternatively, return a `Response` directly from the page:

```astro
if (!post) {
  return new Response(null, { status: 404, statusText: 'Not found' });
}
```

Both work only on routes rendered on demand, and response headers/status must be set at the page level — not inside components or layouts, since headers are already sent by then.

Tag-based invalidation purges *all* cached entries whose tags include any of the provided tags, while path-based invalidation targets a single URL and is exact-match only (no glob or wildcard support). Choose tags when content spans multiple routes or shares a logical grouping—e.g. tagging every product page `products` lets you purge them all at once after a catalog update. Choose `path` when you need to invalidate one specific URL, such as revalidating `/api/data` alone. You can combine both strategies in one `invalidate()` call depending on what changed:

```ts
await context.cache.invalidate({ tags: ['data'] });
await context.cache.invalidate({ path: '/api/data' });
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Create a config helper that returns a `CacheProviderConfig` object with an `entrypoint` pointing to your runtime module and a `config` property containing the serialized options. Then call the helper in `astro.config.mjs` and pass the result to `cache.provider`.

```ts
// my-provider/config.ts
import type { CacheProviderConfig } from 'astro';
interface MyProviderOptions { apiKey: string; region?: string; }
export function myCache(options: MyProviderOptions): CacheProviderConfig {
  return {
    entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime',
    config: options,
  };
}
```

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { myCache } from 'my-provider/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: { provider: myCache({ apiKey: '...', region: 'us-east-1' }) },
});
```

The runtime module default-exports a `CacheProviderFactory` that receives the serialized `config` and returns a `CacheProvider`.

Add these scripts to your `package.json`:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "astro dev",
    "build": "astro build",
    "preview": "astro preview"
  }
}
```
Forward flags through npm with `--` separator: `npm run dev -- --port 8080`. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cli-reference/

Enable `adapterFeatures: { staticHeaders: true }` in `setAdapter()`. Astro then provides a `routeToHeaders` map in the `astro:build:generated` hook containing headers for static pages (e.g., CSP). Use this map to generate platform-specific header files like `_headers` or framework config.

```js
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  adapterFeatures: { staticHeaders: true },
});
```

```js
// In hook
'astro:build:generated': ({ routeToHeaders }) => {
  // generate platform header config from routeToHeaders
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Set the logger's level to `'silent'` — the `AstroLoggerLevel` value that enables silent logging (equivalent to the `--silent` CLI flag) — using the `logger` option added in `astro@7.0.0`:

```js
import { defineConfig, logHandlers } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  logger: logHandlers.json({ level: 'silent' })
});
```

Built-in handlers take a `level` option; handler-specific options depend on the configured logger, or you can supply your own handler via `logger.entrypoint` plus `logger.config` (e.g. `config: { level: 'silent' }`).

No. Vite's built-in `connection` event fires **before** Dev Toolbar apps are initialized, so messages sent in response to it may reach the app before it can receive them. Instead, use `toolbar.onAppInitialized()` in your `astro:server:setup` hook, which guarantees the app is fully initialized before you send:

```js
'astro:server:setup': ({ toolbar }) => {
  toolbar.onAppInitialized('my-app', () => {
    toolbar.send('server-message', { message: 'Hello!' });
  });
},
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/dev-toolbar-app-reference/

Pass `locals` in the second argument (`ContainerRenderOptions`) of `renderToString()` or `renderToResponse()`; its type is `App.Locals`, and the component reads it via `Astro.locals` as usual.

```js
const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(Card, {
  locals: {
    checkAuth() {
      return true;
    },
  },
});
```

In the component, `const { checkAuth } = Astro.locals;` then returns the injected value, so the same component can be rendered authenticated or unauthenticated by passing different `locals` per call.

Use the `glob()` loader from `astro/loaders`, passing a `pattern` of glob matches and a `base` directory:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { glob } from 'astro/loaders';

const pages = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/data/pages" }),
});

export const collections = { pages };
```

The `pattern` must be written relative to the `base` directory (e.g. `"**/*.md"`, not `"src/data/pages/**/*.md"`), since `base` is the directory from which `pattern` is resolved; it defaults to `"."`. Patterns use micromatch glob syntax, and each entry's `id` is auto-generated from its file name.

The `logger` option in your `astro.config.mjs` should use `logHandlers.json()` with `pretty: true` and `level: 'warn'`.

```js
import { defineConfig, logHandlers } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  logger: logHandlers.json({ pretty: true, level: 'warn' })
});
```

This configuration will output JSON logs in a multi-line format and only print messages with the `warn` or `error` level.

`"auto"` (recommended): you provide a valid module via `serverEntrypoint` or Vite `rollupOptions.input`; Astro handles resolution. `"explicit"` (deprecated, default in v6): you must export host-required values using `createExports()` in the server entrypoint and pass them via `exports` to `setAdapter()`. New adapters should use `"auto"`; `"explicit"` supports legacy v5 adapters only.

```js
// auto (recommended)
setAdapter({
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
});

// explicit (deprecated)
setAdapter({
  entrypointResolution: 'explicit',
  exports: createExports({ ... }),
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

A team should pin the Astro version and regularly update to stay current with experimental feature changes, since these flags may introduce breaking changes even in patch releases. It's important to monitor the Astro changelog and test updates thoroughly when new versions are released.

For example:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  experimental: {
    // enable specific experimental features
  },
});
```

Teams should also document which experimental flags are in use, review them periodically, and plan for migration when features become stable. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/

Both are `AdapterSupportsKind` values (added in `astro@5.0.0`) used in `supportedAstroFeatures`: `"limited"` means the adapter supports a subset of the full feature — use it when the feature works but not in all cases or with all options, typically paired with a `message` object explaining the constraints in the CLI. `"unsupported"` means the feature isn't supported and users may encounter build issues — use it to warn them upfront. Any value can be an object `{ support, message }`.

```js
supportedAstroFeatures: {
  sharpImageService: {
    support: "limited",
    message: 'This adapter supports the built-in sharp image service, but with some limitations.'
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

With `fontProviders.local()`, `options.variants` is required: each variant represents a `@font-face` declaration and must include a `src` (paths relative to the project root, a package import, a URL, or objects with a `tech` value). `weight` and `style` are optional and are inferred from the first source when omitted; `weight` may be a range string for variable fonts (`"100 900"`). Each variant may also set `display`, `unicodeRange`, `stretch`, `featureSettings`, and `variationSettings`.

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.local(),
    name: "Custom",
    cssVariable: "--font-custom",
    options: {
      variants: [
        { weight: 400, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-400.woff2"] },
        { weight: 700, style: "normal", src: ["./src/assets/fonts/custom-700.woff2"] }
      ]
    }
  }]
});
```

Store font files outside `public/` (e.g. in `src/`) to avoid duplicated build output.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

In Astro 6+, use the `'astro:build:done'` hook to access the resolved i18n configuration via `ssrManifest.i18n.strategy`. This field contains the strategy string (e.g., `"domains-prefix-always"`) as defined in your project's config.

Example:

```js
hooks: {
  'astro:build:done': ({ ssrManifest }) => {
    const strategy = ssrManifest?.i18n?.strategy;
    if (strategy === 'domains-prefix-always') {
      // Handle domain-based routing logic
    }
  }
}
```

