Manually import renderers when the container runs at runtime or inside other "shells" (e.g. PHP, Ruby, Java), and for on-demand rendered applications — cases where the `astro:container` virtual module helpers aren't available. Automatic loading only covers static applications or containers not called at runtime (e.g. testing with `vitest`).

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import reactRenderer from "@astrojs/react/server.js";
import vueRenderer from "@astrojs/vue/server.js";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
container.addServerRenderer({ renderer: vueRenderer });
container.addServerRenderer({ renderer: reactRenderer });
container.addClientRenderer({
  name: "@astrojs/react",
  entrypoint: "@astrojs/react/client.js",
});
```

Add all server renderers before client renderers; client renderers are only needed to hydrate `client:*` directives.

Pass the `params` option to `renderToString()`:

```js
import { experimental_AstroContainer } from "astro/container";
import LocaleSlug from "../src/pages/[locale]/[slug].astro";

const container = await experimental_AstroContainer.create();
const result = await container.renderToString(LocaleSlug, {
  params: {
    locale: "en",
    slug: "getting-started",
  },
});
```

The `params` option (`Record<string, string | undefined>`) is intended for components that generate dynamic routes, and resolves `Astro.params` inside the component during rendering.

No — use the built-in `file()` loader pointed at a JSON (or YAML/TOML) file holding your array:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { file } from "astro/loaders";
const dogs = defineCollection({
  loader: file("src/data/dogs.json"),
});
export const collections = { dogs };
```

Each object must include a unique `id` key, because `file()` does not auto-generate IDs (unlike `glob()`). Build a custom object loader with `load()` and `store.set()` only when you need custom fetching or transformation logic.

Use the `GetStaticPaths` type utility (added in Astro 2.1.0) with `satisfies` to infer `Astro.params` and `Astro.props` types. Import `GetStaticPaths`, `InferGetStaticParamsType`, and `InferGetStaticPropsType` from `"astro"`.

```astro
---import type { GetStaticPaths, InferGetStaticParamsType, InferGetStaticPropsType } from "astro";
export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
  const posts = await getCollection("blog");
  return posts.map(post => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
    props: { draft: post.data.draft, title: post.data.title }
  }));
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;
type Params = InferGetStaticParamsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;
type Props = InferGetStaticPropsType<typeof getStaticPaths>;
const { id } = Astro.params; // { id: string }
const { title } = Astro.props; // { draft: boolean; title: string }---
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/typescript/

Type your provider with `FontProvider<FamilyOptions | undefined>` where `FamilyOptions` declares `minimal?: boolean`, receive `options` in `resolveFont`, then enable it per font family in the config:

```ts
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: registryFontProvider(),
    options: { minimal: true },
    name: "Custom",
    cssVariable: "--font-custom"
  }]
});
```

The `options` object in each `fonts` entry is what flows into the provider's typed family options.

Structure your package with two parts:

1. **Config helper** — a function users call in `astro.config.mjs` that returns a `CacheProviderConfig` (`{ entrypoint, config }`). This tells Astro where to find the runtime module and what options to pass.

2. **Runtime module** — a file that default-exports a `CacheProviderFactory` function. This module is bundled into the SSR output, so it must be runtime-agnostic (avoid Node.js built-ins unless your target runtime supports them).

```ts
// my-provider/config.ts
import type { CacheProviderConfig } from 'astro';
export function myCache(options): CacheProviderConfig {
  return { entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime', config: options };
}
```

```ts
// my-provider/runtime.ts
import type { CacheProviderFactory } from 'astro';
const factory: CacheProviderFactory = (config) => {
  return { name: 'my-cache-provider', async invalidate() {} };
};
export default factory;
```

Users then register it via `cache: { provider: myCache({...}) }` in their Astro config.

`getStaticPaths()` is the wrong tool for this route because it exists to generate prerendered routes at build time; in `server` mode it only takes effect if you explicitly opt in with `export const prerender = true`. An on-demand route already resolves `params` per request — any value matching the dynamic pattern — so enumerating entries up front adds nothing, and its `props` are meant for data passed to prerendered templates. Fetch the extra data per request from the param instead:

```astro
---
import { getPost } from '../api';
const post = await getPost(Astro.params.id);
if (!post) return Astro.redirect('/404');
---
<h1>{post.name}</h1>
```

If you actually want these pages built statically, add `export const prerender = true` and keep `getStaticPaths()` (its `params` must be strings, since they're URL-encoded).

To include specific glyphs with the Google font provider, configure `fontProviders.google()` with `options.experimental.glyphs` as an array of character strings to limit which glyphs are downloaded. For example:
```js
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.google(),
    name: "Inter",
    cssVariable: "--font-inter",
    options: { experimental: { glyphs: ["a", "b", "c"] } }
  }]
});
```
This is an experimental Google provider option in Astro 7.

Set `build.serverEntry` (default `'entry.mjs'`):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    serverEntry: 'server.mjs'
  }
});
```

It only names the server entrypoint file emitted when building to SSR — it does not affect client build output. Keep the `.mjs` extension so the runtime detects the file as a JavaScript module. Note that this value is usually set by your adapter for the target host, so an adapter's value may need to be overridden.

The `getStaticPaths()` function runs in an isolated scope at build time. It cannot reference variables or functions from its parent component scope—only imported modules are allowed. The compiler emits a warning if you violate this rule.

```astro
---// ❌ This breaks: 'data' is from parent scope
const data = await fetchData();
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return data.map(...); // Compiler warning
}
// ✅ This works: import at top level
import { fetchData } from "../utils/data";
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const data = await fetchData();
  return data.map(...);
}---
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

You should use an **external image service** rather than a local one, since your CDN handles optimization via RIAPI-style URLs.

An external service is ideal because it simply maps input options to a remote URL without needing to transform images locally. This avoids unnecessary build-time processing and leverages the CDN's existing capabilities.

Example:
```ts
import type { ExternalImageService, ImageTransform } from "astro";

const service: ExternalImageService = {
  getURL(options, imageConfig) {
    const config = imageConfig.service.config;
    return `${config.cdnUrl}/${options.src}?q=${options.quality}&w=${options.width}&h=${options.height}`;
  },
  getHTMLAttributes(options) {
    const { src, format, quality, ...attributes } = options;
    return {
      ...attributes,
      loading: 'lazy',
      decoding: 'async'
    };
  }
};

export default service;
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/image-service-reference/

To write an Astro endpoint that processes an image using your configured image service’s `parseURL()` and `transform()` methods, use `getConfiguredImageService()` to get the service instance, then call `parseURL()` on the request URL. Fetch the image buffer from the parsed source, transform it with `imageService.transform()`, and return the result as a Response with the correct Content-Type header.

Example:
```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { getConfiguredImageService, imageConfig } from "astro:assets";
import { isLocalService } from "astro/assets";
import * as mime from "mrmime";

export const GET: APIRoute = async ({ request, logger }) => {
  const imageService = await getConfiguredImageService();
  if (!isLocalService(imageService)) {
    return new Response("This endpoint only works with a local image service.", { status: 400 });
  }

  const imageTransform = await imageService.parseURL(new URL(request.url), imageConfig, logger);
  if (!imageTransform) {
    return new Response("Invalid image URL.", { status: 400 });
  }

  // Fetch the image buffer from imageTransform.src
  const inputBuffer = await fetch(imageTransform.src).then(res => res.arrayBuffer());

  const { data, format } = await imageService.transform(
    new Uint8Array(inputBuffer),
    imageTransform,
    imageConfig,
    logger
  );

  return new Response(new Uint8Array(data), {
    status: 200,
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": mime.lookup(format) || "",
    },
  });
};
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/image-service-reference/

Pass a config object with the token when calling the provider factory in the `fonts` array of your Astro config:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import { registryFontProvider } from "./font-provider";

export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: registryFontProvider({ token: "..." }),
    name: "Custom",
    cssVariable: "--font-custom"
  }]
});
```

The factory accepts a config (e.g. `interface Config { token: string }`) and exposes it on the returned provider as `config`, so `init()` can use it to authenticate requests to the private registry.

No. Use `app.onToolbarPlacementUpdated()` instead — it fires whenever the user moves the Dev Toolbar and is explicitly intended for repositioning your app's UI:

```js
import { defineToolbarApp } from "astro/toolbar";
export default defineToolbarApp({
  init(canvas, app) {
    app.onToolbarPlacementUpdated((options) => {
      console.log(`The toolbar is now placed at ${options.placement}!`);
    });
  },
});
```

The callback receives `{placement: 'bottom-left' | 'bottom-center' | 'bottom-right'}` (added in `astro@4.7.0`). `toggleState()` only enables/disables the app; it does not trigger repositioning.

Implement a custom session driver with two files: a config file returning `SessionDriverConfig` with an `entrypoint` to your runtime file, and a runtime file exporting a default function returning a `SessionDriver` object that uses a simple key-value store (e.g., `Map`, file system, or database) instead of Redis. Follow the same pattern as the memory driver example but replace `LRUCache` with your store.

driver/config.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriverConfig } from 'astro'
export interface Config { /* your options */ }
export function kvDriver(config: Config = {}): SessionDriverConfig {
  return { entrypoint: new URL('./runtime.js', import.meta.url), config }
}
```

driver/runtime.ts:
```ts
import type { SessionDriver } from 'astro'
import type { Config } from './config'
const store = new Map<string, any>()
export default function(config: Config): SessionDriver {
  return {
    setItem: async (key, value) => { store.set(key, value) },
    getItem: async (key) => { return store.get(key) },
    removeItem: async (key) => { store.delete(key) },
  }
}
```

Register in `astro.config.ts`:
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config'
import { kvDriver } from './driver/config'
export default defineConfig({ session: { driver: kvDriver({ /* options */ }) } })
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/session-driver-reference/

The string form only sets the cookie name; every other option stays at its default (`sameSite: 'lax'`, `httpOnly: true`, `secure: true`). The object form sets `name` along with any other `AstroCookieSetOptions`, which are merged with those defaults. So if you need to change `sameSite` or `secure`, you must use the object form:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    cookie: {
      name: "my-session-cookie",
      sameSite: "lax",
      secure: true,
    }
  }
});
```

(`session.cookie` was added in `astro@5.7.0`.)

The `glob()` loader's default `generateId()` produced it: it uses `github-slugger` to create a kebab-cased slug from the file name, so `Jane-Doe.json` becomes `jane-doe`. Keep the original casing by passing a custom `generateId` that strips only the extension:

```ts
const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({
    pattern: "**/*.json",
    base: "./src/data/authors",
    generateId: ({ entry }) => entry.replace(/\.json$/, ""),
  }),
});
```

`entry` is the path relative to `base`. Alternatively, override a single entry's id by adding a `slug` property to that JSON data object (or Markdown frontmatter).

Since Astro v7.3.0 every image service hook (`validateOptions()`, `getURL()`, `parseURL()`, `transform()`, `getHTMLAttributes()`) receives a `logger` argument you can use for warnings and errors:

```ts
import type { LocalImageService } from 'astro';
const service: LocalImageService = {
  async transform(inputBuffer, transform, imageConfig, logger) {
    logger.warn(`Could not optimize "${transform.src}". Passing it through unchanged.`);
    return { data: inputBuffer, format: 'png' };
  },
};
```

Returning a `format` from `transform()` ensures the correct MIME type is served in dev and on-demand rendering.

Pass `format` in the `paginate()` options (added in Astro 7.1.0) as a function that receives the computed URL and returns a transformed one, letting you reshape URLs before rendering:

```js
return paginate(allPokemon, {
  pageSize: 10,
  format: (url) => `${url}.html`,
});
```

Other options are `pageSize` (default 10), `params`, and `props`.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

`markdown.remarkPlugins` is replaced by `markdown.processor` (added in astro@6.4.0). To use remark-toc, install `@astrojs/markdown-remark` and pass `unified()` as the processor:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import remarkToc from 'remark-toc';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [[remarkToc, { heading: "contents" }]],
    }),
  },
});
```

Plugins with options are passed as a nested array: the plugin first, then an options object.

In `astro.config.mjs`, set `image.service` to an object with an `entrypoint` (a built-in service such as `'astro/assets/services/sharp'`, a third-party package, or your own) and an optional `config` object of service-specific settings:

```js
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    service: {
      entrypoint: "your-entrypoint",
      config: {
        // ... service-specific config. Optional.
      },
    },
  },
});
```

Your service reads these values at runtime via `imageConfig.service.config`.

The `types` string must export an `Entry` type or interface — its contents are written verbatim into a generated TypeScript file, and a missing `Entry` export makes that file invalid. For example:

```ts
createSchema: async () => {
  const schema = await getSchema();
  const types = await getTypes();
  return {
    schema,
    types: `export type Entry = ${types}`,
  };
},
```

If type generation itself fails, `astro sync` reports a `GenerateContentTypesError`.

A `: Loader` return annotation checks only the returned value; it does not provide type safety inside your loader object, so `store`, `parseData`, and the schema lose inference. Use the TypeScript `satisfies` operator on the returned object instead — it validates the object against `Loader` while keeping inference inside `load` and preserving type inference when the loader is used in a collection:

```ts
import type { Loader } from "astro/loaders";

export function feedLoader(options: { url: string }) {
  return {
    name: "feed-loader",
    load: async ({ store, parseData }) => {
      const data = await parseData({ id, data: item });
      store.set({ id, data });
    },
  } satisfies Loader;
}
```

Since Astro 6, `satisfies Loader` is also required when you provide a `schema`, because collection types are now inferred rather than generated with `zod-to-ts`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

