Export a function that returns a `FontProvider` object; for a private registry, fetch the font data in `init()`, expose the family names with `listFonts()`, and look up faces by name in `resolveFont()`:

```ts
import type { FontProvider } from "astro";
import { retrieveFonts, type Fonts } from "./utils.js";

export function registryFontProvider(): FontProvider {
  let data: Fonts = {};
  return {
    name: "registry",
    init: async () => { data = await retrieveFonts(token); },
    listFonts: () => Object.keys(data),
    resolveFont: ({ familyName, ...rest }) => {
      const fonts = data[familyName];
      if (fonts) return { fonts };
      return undefined;
    },
  };
}
```

Register it in the config's `fonts` array (`provider: registryFontProvider()`, plus `name` and `cssVariable`). Pass config for a token, or use the `FontProvider<FamilyOptions | undefined>` generic for per-family options.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/font-provider-reference/

Dynamic values in a route's `prerender` export were removed in Astro v5 — only static `true` and `false` are supported — so in Astro 7 you set prerendering from an integration's `astro:route:setup` hook (note this is not `setPrerenderer`, which installs a custom prerenderer):

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { loadEnv } from 'vite';
export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    {
      name: 'set-prerender',
      hooks: {
        'astro:route:setup': ({ route }) => {
          const { PRERENDER } = loadEnv(process.env.NODE_ENV, process.cwd(), '');
          if (route.component.endsWith('/blog/[slug].astro')) {
            route.prerender = PRERENDER;
          }
        },
      },
    },
  ],
});
```

Match routes by `route.component` and assign `route.prerender` as needed.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/integrations-reference/

All of them: the `context` object passed as the first argument to endpoint functions (typed `APIContext`) and to middleware mirrors the properties of the `Astro` global available in `.astro` frontmatter. So `props`, `params`, `request`, and `redirect()` exist in both places — as `Astro.props`, `Astro.params`, `Astro.request`, `Astro.redirect()` in a page's frontmatter, and as destructured arguments in an endpoint:

```ts
import type { APIContext } from 'astro';
export function GET({ props, params, request, redirect }: APIContext) {
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ id: params.id, author: props.author }));
}
```

`props` and `params` can be populated by `getStaticPaths()` on static routes. Runtime distinctions: on prerendered pages `request.url` omits search parameters (they're present on-demand), and `redirect(path, status)` supports status codes only on-demand — prerendered routes emit a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` tag instead (default `302` on-demand).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

It fails because your runtime module is bundled into your SSR output and must be runtime-agnostic: non-Node runtimes like Cloudflare Workers don't provide Node.js built-in modules, so importing `node:fs` (or `node:path`) will error at runtime. The correct pattern is to keep the runtime module free of filesystem access and instead pass initialization data through the config helper—the `config` object you return from `myCache()` is serialized and delivered to the `CacheProviderFactory` at runtime:

```ts
export function myCache(options: MyProviderOptions): CacheProviderConfig {
  return {
    entrypoint: 'my-provider/runtime',
    config: options, // passed to the factory at runtime
  };
}
```

Load any JSON index at config time (in `astro.config.mjs`) and embed it in `config`, or fetch it remotely inside the factory. On Cloudflare, bindings and env vars come from `wrangler.jsonc` rather than the filesystem.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Set `query.sort` to `false` in the `memoryCache()` options; it defaults to `true`, which alphabetically sorts query parameters so both orderings resolve to one cache entry:

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  cache: {
    provider: memoryCache({ query: { sort: false } }),
  },
});
```

With sorting disabled, `/page?b=2&a=1` and `/page?a=1&b=2` are cached as separate entries. Note that tracking-parameter exclusion still applies by default—use `query.exclude: []` if those parameters must also contribute to the key.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/cache-provider-reference/

Set `middlewareMode: 'edge'` in `adapterFeatures` and consume the `astro:build:ssr` hook. The hook provides `middlewareEntryPoint` (a URL to the physical file), which will be `undefined` if the feature isn't opted into. Use it to emit an edge middleware file via your bundler.

```js
'adapterFeatures': { middlewareMode: 'edge' },
// ...
'astro:build:ssr': ({ middlewareEntryPoint }) => {
  if (middlewareEntryPoint) {
    createEdgeMiddleware(middlewareEntryPoint)
  }
}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

`fallbackType: "redirect"` (default) sends a 301/302 response — the browser navigates to the fallback URL and the address bar updates. `fallbackType: "rewrite"` serves the fallback page's content at the original URL — the address bar stays on the requested path (e.g., `/fr/about/`) while showing `/en/about/` content.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    defaultLocale: "en",
    locales: ["en", "fr"],
    fallback: { fr: "en" },
    routing: { fallbackType: "rewrite" }
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Enable the flag under the `experimental` key in your Astro configuration file with `defineConfig`:

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  experimental: {
    incrementalBuild: true,
  },
});
```

Boolean flags are set to `true`; some flags take a typed value or helper instead (e.g. `collectionStorage: "chunked"`, or `svgOptimizer: svgoOptimizer()` imported from `astro/config`). The feature is unavailable until its flag is set, and flags can be removed once the feature stabilizes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/experimental-flags/

Pass a `Request` as the rewrite payload, constructing it with the target URL and a `headers` option; in a page you'd write:

```astro
---
return Astro.rewrite(new Request(new URL("../", Astro.url), {
  headers: {
    "x-custom-header": JSON.stringify(Astro.locals.someValue)
  }
}));
---
```

The same works from an endpoint via `context.rewrite()`. These headers are sent with the request that renders the parent page, so middleware and the parent route see them. Note that `Astro.rewrite()` cannot be used after the request body has been read — clone the request first (`Astro.request.clone().formData()`).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/api-reference/

Register the renderer from an integration's `astro:config:setup` hook via `addRenderer({ name, clientEntrypoint, serverEntrypoint })`: Astro imports the server module during development and builds, while the client module is imported only in the browser to hydrate components carrying a `client:*` directive, so no client JavaScript loads unless you explicitly mark a component interactive. The client entrypoint default-exports a function receiving the island element and returning an async hydrator:

```ts
export default (element: HTMLElement) =>
  async (
    Component: any,
    props: Record<string, unknown>,
    slots: Record<string, string>,
    { client }: { client: string },
  ) => {
    const rendered = Component({ ...props, slots });
    if (client === 'only') {
      element.innerHTML = '';
    }
    const node = document.createElement(rendered.tag);
    node.textContent = rendered.text;
    element.appendChild(node);
    element.addEventListener('astro:unmount', () => node.remove(), { once: true });
  };
```

Missing `clientEntrypoint` with a `client:*` directive raises `NoClientEntrypoint`, and `client:only` requires a renderer hint (e.g. `client:only="react"`) or `NoClientOnlyHint` is thrown.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/renderer-reference/

`FamilyOptions` defines the per-family options your provider accepts, so they are typed when passed through the config. Declare the interface, parameterize the provider as `FontProvider<FamilyOptions | undefined>`, and `resolveFont` receives `options` typed as `FamilyOptions | undefined`:

```ts
interface FamilyOptions { minimal?: boolean; }
export function registryFontProvider(): FontProvider<FamilyOptions | undefined> { /* ... */ }
```

Users then set these per font family in `astro.config.ts` via `options: { minimal: true }`.

Export a default `LocalImageService<AstroConfig["image"]>` object with `getURL()`, `parseURL()`, `transform()`, optionally `getHTMLAttributes()` and `propertiesToHash`. `getURL()` encodes `href`/`w`/`h`/`q`/`f` query params and points either to the built-in `/_image` endpoint or your own; `parseURL()` decodes them back into an options object; `transform()` runs your library and must return `{ data, format }`.

For your own endpoint (`src/api/my_custom_endpoint_that_transforms_images.ts`):

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { isLocalService } from "astro/assets";
import { getConfiguredImageService, imageConfig } from "astro:assets";

export const GET: APIRoute = async ({ request, logger }) => {
  const imageService = await getConfiguredImageService();
  if (!isLocalService(imageService)) {
    return new Response("This endpoint only works with a local image service.", { status: 400 });
  }
  const imageTransform = await imageService.parseURL(new URL(request.url), imageConfig, logger);
  if (!imageTransform) return new Response("Invalid image URL.", { status: 400 });
  const inputBuffer = await getImageBuffer(imageTransform.src);
  const { data, format } = await imageService.transform(inputBuffer, imageTransform, imageConfig, logger);
  return new Response(new Uint8Array(data), { status: 200, headers: { "Content-Type": mime.lookup(format) || "" } });
};
```

At build time only `transform()` runs; dev/on-demand goes `getURL()` → endpoint → `parseURL()` → `transform()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/image-service-reference/

Generate a digest from the entry data with `generateDigest()` and pass it as `digest` when calling `store.set()`; `set()` compares it against the stored entry's digest and returns `false` when they match, skipping the update:

```ts
load: async ({ store, parseData, generateDigest }) => {
  for (const item of feed.items) {
    const data = await parseData({ id: item.guid, data: item });
    const digest = generateDigest(data);
    store.set({ id: item.guid, data, digest }); // false if unchanged
  }
}
```

`generateDigest()` (added in `astro@5.0.0`) produces a non-cryptographic content digest of an object or string; the digest must be a string that changes whenever the data changes. The format is up to the loader.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Define each collection with `defineCollection()` in `src/content.config.ts`, passing a `schema` built from Zod types imported from `astro/zod` (a re-export supporting all of Zod 4):

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";
import { glob } from "astro/loaders";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    updatedDate: z.coerce.date().optional(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog };
```

Every frontmatter property must then exist in the declared form; violations produce a helpful error, and the schema powers generated TypeScript types and autocompletion when querying. Zod methods such as `.parse()` and `.transform()` work, except `image().refine()`, which is unsupported. Restart the dev server or sync the content layer (`s + enter`) so Astro picks up schema changes.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/

Set `image.domains` in `astro.config.mjs` to an array of exact permitted domain names, or `image.remotePatterns` to an array of URL patterns (protocol, hostname, port, pathname), when you need wildcards:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['astro.build'],
  },
});
```

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    remotePatterns: [{
      protocol: 'https',
      hostname: '**.amazonaws.com',
    }],
  },
});
```

`image.domains` accepts exact domain names only — wildcards are not permitted; use `image.remotePatterns` for `**.`/`*.` hostname and `/**`/`/*` pathname wildcards. Remote images outside these lists are not optimized by Astro, though `<Image />` can still render them to prevent CLS. Optimizing an unlisted remote image throws “Remote image is not allowed.”

Add a global stylesheet that styles form controls with `:focus-visible`, replacing (never merely removing) the browser’s default focus ring:

```css
button:focus-visible,
a:focus-visible,
[role="button"]:focus-visible,
input:focus-visible,
select:focus-visible,
textarea:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid #0b5fff;
  outline-offset: 2px;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
```

The indicator must have at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colours (WCAG 1.4.11), be at least 2 CSS pixels thick (WCAG 2.4.13), and be offset so it does not disappear into the control border. Shipping `*:focus { outline: none; }` without an equal or better replacement fails WCAG 2.4.7 (Level AA). Pair `:focus-visible` with `:focus` if you must support older engines.

Configure i18n routing in `astro.config.mjs`, create localized folders under `src/pages/`, and build links with `getRelativeLocaleUrl()` from `astro:i18n`:

```
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: {
    locales: ["es", "en", "pt-br"],
    defaultLocale: "en",
  }
})
```

Touch target size is not handled by routing — apply CSS to your language switcher and other controls. WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 requires at least 24×24 CSS px (44×44 for the enhanced criterion):

```css
button,
a.button,
[role="button"] {
  min-height: 44px;
  min-width: 44px;
}
```

Inline links inside running prose are exempt.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

A `.d.ts` file is an ambient module declaration and does not allow top-level `import`/`export` statements: if TypeScript encounters one, the file is treated as a module augmentation instead, which breaks your global types. Avoid it by reusing types with dynamic `import()` type syntax inside the declaration rather than top-level imports:

```ts
type Product = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
};
declare namespace App {
  interface Locals {
    orders: Map<string, Product[]>
    session: import("./lib/server/session").Session | null;
    user: import("my-external-library").User;
  }
}
```

Local `type` aliases in the same file are fine — only top-level import/export statements turn the file into a module.

Add top-level declarations with the `declare` keyword in `src/env.d.ts`:

```ts
declare var myString: string;
declare function myFunction(): boolean;
```

This types both `globalThis.myString`/`globalThis.myFunction` and `window.myString`/`window.myFunction`, so the properties are typed in server and client code — prefer `declare var` over `interface Window`, which only types the `window` object. Environment caveats: `window` exists only in client-side code, while `globalThis` is available both server-side and client-side, but its server-side value is not shared with the client. You can also include Astro's own types with `/// <reference path="../.astro/types.d.ts" />`.

Add the remote image’s domain to `image.domains`, or a matching pattern to `image.remotePatterns`, in `astro.config.mjs` so the URL is permitted:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ["astro.build"],
  },
});
```

The error “Remote image is not allowed” means the URL does not match any configured `image.domains` entry or `image.remotePatterns` entry. `image.domains` takes exact domain strings with no wildcards; use `image.remotePatterns` for pattern-based allowlisting (e.g. `hostname: '**.amazonaws.com'`). HTTP redirects are followed — the final destination URL must also be among the allowed patterns. A separate “Failed to retrieve remote image dimensions” error indicates an incorrect URL, not a configuration mismatch.

Astro has built-in TypeScript support: you can import `.ts`/`.tsx` files, write TypeScript directly in component frontmatter, and use `astro.config.ts`. Essentials:

- Keep `tsconfig.json` and extend a template — `"extends": "astro/tsconfigs/base"` (use `strict` or `strictest` if you write TS), plus `"include": [".astro/types.d.ts", "**/*"]` and `"exclude": ["dist"]`.
- Type component props with a `Props` interface in frontmatter; the Astro VS Code extension picks it up automatically:

```astro
---
interface Props {
  name: string;
  greeting?: string;
}
const { greeting = "Hello", name } = Astro.props;
---
<h2>{greeting}, {name}!</h2>
```

- No props: `type Props = Record<string, never>`; required children: `type Props = { children: any; }`.
- The dev server does no type checking — run `astro check` to check `.astro` and `.ts` files (`tsc` ignores `.astro` files entirely).

Enabling `nodejs_compat` in Wrangler lets you use `node:*` imports (e.g., `node:crypto`, `node:path`) in Cloudflare Workers, but pitfalls include: (1) incomplete Node API coverage — some modules are polyfilled, others unavailable; (2) increased Worker size and cold-start latency from polyfills; (3) packages that use non-`node:*` imports (e.g., `require('crypto')`) will fail unless they support the `node:*` syntax. Mitigate by: using `node:*` imports exclusively, checking Cloudflare's supported API list, avoiding dependencies that don't use `node:*`, and testing locally with `wrangler dev`. If a package fails, find an alternative or vendor the dependency.

```jsonc
// wrangler.jsonc
{
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"]
}
```

```js
// Server-side code
import { Buffer } from 'node:buffer';
import { randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/

Define actions with `accept: 'form'` and validate submissions through the `input` schema; Astro parses form data into an object keyed by each input's `name`, and your schema validates that object:

```ts
import { defineAction } from 'astro:actions';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';

export const server = {
  newsletter: defineAction({
    accept: 'form',
    input: z.object({
      email: z.email(),
      terms: z.boolean(),
    }),
    handler: async ({ email, terms }) => { /* ... */ },
  })
}
```

Omit `input` if the handler needs raw `FormData`. Submit via `<form method="POST" action={actions.newsletter}>` or call `actions.newsletter(formData)` from a client script. Read the result with `Astro.getActionResult(actions.newsletter)`; use `isInputError(result?.error)` to access per-field errors in `result.error.fields`. To preserve values on resubmit, add `transition:persist` to inputs and enable view transitions.

Set `preserveBuildClientDir: true` (added in astro@6.0.0) and/or `preserveBuildServerDir: true` (added in astro@6.4.0) in `adapterFeatures` when calling `setAdapter()`; both default to `false`. A use case is a static site containing server islands or image endpoints that still need a server entry, requiring a consistent `dist/client/` and `dist/server/` layout:

```js
setAdapter({
  name: '@example/my-adapter',
  entrypointResolution: 'auto',
  serverEntrypoint: '@example/my-adapter/server.js',
  adapterFeatures: {
    preserveBuildServerDir: true,
  },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/adapter-reference/

Endpoints support the same dynamic routing as pages: name the file with a bracketed parameter and export `getStaticPaths()`, then read the value from `params` inside `GET`.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const GET = (({ params, request }) => {
  const id = Number(params.id);
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ name: usernames[id] }));
}) satisfies APIRoute;
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [{ params: { id: "0" } }, { params: { id: "1" } }];
}
```

In static mode each entry is generated at build time (`/api/0.json`, `/api/1.json`); with on-demand rendering, passing `props` is not supported since an endpoint is a function, not a component. Trailing slashes: endpoints whose URLs include a file extension (e.g. `/sitemap.xml`) can only be accessed without a trailing slash, regardless of your `build.trailingSlash` configuration.

Install `rollup-plugin-visualizer` as a dev dependency, then add it under `vite.plugins` in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { visualizer } from "rollup-plugin-visualizer";
export default defineConfig({vite: {    plugins: [visualizer({        emitFile: true,        filename: "stats.html",    })]}});
```

Run `npm run build` (or `pnpm build`/`yarn build`) and open `stats.html` at the root of `dist/`. With on-demand rendering there are two files: one in `dist/client/` and one in `dist/server/`. Caveats: the visualizer shows all dependencies used across the site but not per-page sizes, and due to Astro's hydration model the build isn't necessarily what the client receives.

Add `src/pages/llms-full.txt.ts` exporting an `GET` that returns the concatenated markdown as a `Response`; in static mode Astro calls it at build time and writes `/llms-full.txt`, in SSR it runs on request.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const GET = (async () => {
  const body = "# Example Corp — full content\n\n> All public documentation, concatenated.\n\n---\n\n# Getting started\n...";
  return new Response(body, {
    headers: { "Content-Type": "text/markdown; charset=utf-8" },
  });
}) satisfies APIRoute;
```

Curate the same pages as `llms.txt`, concatenate them in the same order, separate sections with `---` and `#` headings (include each page URL near its heading), strip front-matter, and set cache headers since the file changes on every doc update.

Use `passthroughImageService()` when your adapter does not support Astro's built-in Sharp optimization (e.g. Cloudflare), so you can still use `<Image />` and `<Picture />`. It is a no-op: Astro performs no image transformation, but you keep `astro:assets` benefits—no Cumulative Layout Shift, enforced `alt`, and a consistent authoring experience.

```js
import { defineConfig, passthroughImageService } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    service: passthroughImageService()
  }
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/images/

Set Clerk's `clerkMiddleware()` as the `onRequest` handler in `src/middleware.ts`, then gate each route via `Astro.locals.auth()`:

```ts
import { clerkMiddleware } from "@clerk/astro/server";
export const onRequest = clerkMiddleware({
  /* options */
});
```

```astro
---
const { isAuthenticated, redirectToSignIn } = Astro.locals.auth();
if (!isAuthenticated) return redirectToSignIn();
---
<h1>Dashboard</h1>
```

API routes read the same state and can return `new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 })`. Requests must render on demand (`export const prerender = false` when not in `server` mode). Astro has no official auth solution; Better Auth is the other common choice.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

Create a catch-all API route at `src/pages/api/auth/[...all].ts` that exports an `ALL` handler delegating to your auth library's request handler:

```ts
import { auth } from "../../../lib/auth"; // import your Better Auth instance
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
export const prerender = false; // Not needed in 'server' mode
export const ALL: APIRoute = async (ctx) => {
  return auth.handler(ctx.request);
};
```

This mounts Better Auth at `/api/auth/`, handling sign-in and OAuth flows. To protect your own endpoints instead, check `locals.auth()` in the handler and return a 401 response when unauthenticated.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/authentication/

Export a `GET` function from a file in `src/pages` that returns a `Response` (or a `Promise` resolving to one). Returning anything else throws **EndpointDidNotReturnAResponse**.

```ts
import type { APIContext } from 'astro';
export async function GET({ request, url, cookies }: APIContext): Promise<Response> {
  return Response.json({
    success: true,
    result: 'Data from Astro Endpoint!'
  })
}
```

The `APIContext` also exposes `params`, `request`, and `cookies` for building the payload.

Create `src/pages/dashboard.astro`, query Firestore in the frontmatter with the Admin SDK, then map the docs to render each record with a details link and an edit button:

```astro
---
import { app } from "../firebase/server";
import { getFirestore } from "firebase-admin/firestore";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

interface Friend {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age: number;
  isBestFriend: boolean;
}

const db = getFirestore(app);
const friendsRef = db.collection("friends");
const friendsSnapshot = await friendsRef.get();
const friends = friendsSnapshot.docs.map((doc) => ({
  id: doc.id,
  ...doc.data(),
})) as Friend[];
---

<Layout title="My friends">
  <h1>Friends</h1>
  <ul>
    {
      friends.map((friend) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/friend/${friend.id}`}>{friend.name}</a>
          <span>({friend.age})</span>
          <strong>{friend.isBestFriend ? "Bestie" : "Friend"}</strong>
          <a href={`/edit/${friend.id}`}>Edit</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</Layout>
```

The edit button is a plain link to `/edit/${friend.id}`, which resolves to `src/pages/edit/[id].astro`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/firebase/

With an SSR adapter configured, an RSS endpoint route renders on demand: create `src/pages/rss.xml.js` that exports an async `GET(context)` function returning the `rss()` result, instead of a prerendered build-time file.

```js
import rss from '@astrojs/rss';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
export async function GET(context) {
  const blog = await getCollection('blog');
  return rss({
    title: 'Buzz’s Blog',
    description: 'A humble Astronaut’s guide to the stars',
    site: context.site,
    items: blog.map((post) => ({
      title: post.data.title,
      pubDate: post.data.pubDate,
      description: post.data.description,
      link: `/blog/${post.id}/`,
    })),
  });
}
```

Optionally apply `rssSchema` to your collection schema to enforce valid item properties.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/rss/

Add a dynamic route at `src/pages/friend/[id].astro` that reads the `id` param, fetches that one Firestore document, and redirects to `/404` when the id is missing or the document doesn't exist:

```astro
---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { app } from "../../firebase/server";
import { getFirestore } from "firebase-admin/firestore";

interface Friend {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  isBestFriend: boolean;
}
const { id } = Astro.params;
if (!id) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}
const db = getFirestore(app);
const friendsRef = db.collection("friends");
const friendSnapshot = await friendsRef.doc(id).get();
if (!friendSnapshot.exists) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}
const friend = friendSnapshot.data() as Friend;
---

<Layout title={friend.name}>
  <h1>{friend.name}</h1>
  <p>Age: {friend.age}</p>
  <p>Is best friend: {friend.isBestFriend ? "Yes" : "No"}</p>
</Layout>
```

This requires the Firebase server setup (`src/firebase/server.ts`) used by the other pages.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/backend/firebase/

`import.meta.glob()` only supports static string literals; dynamic variables and string interpolation are not allowed in the pattern. The common workaround is to glob a larger static set containing all the files you need, then filter the results using the dynamic value:

```astro
---
const { postSlug } = Astro.props;
const pathToMyFeaturedPost = `src/pages/blog/${postSlug}.md`;
const posts = Object.values(
  import.meta.glob("../pages/blog/*.md", { eager: true }),
);
const myFeaturedPost: any = posts.find((post: any) =>
  post.file.includes(pathToMyFeaturedPost),
);
---
```

Pass `{ eager: true }` as the second argument to load all matched modules up front, and use `Object.values()` on the result.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/imports/

Pass values from your component frontmatter to the `<style>` block with the `define:vars` directive; they become CSS custom properties named `--<variableName>` you can reference with `var()`.

```astro
---
const foregroundColor = "rgb(221 243 228)";
const backgroundColor = "rgb(24 121 78)";
---
<style define:vars={{ foregroundColor, backgroundColor }}>
  h1 {
    background-color: var(--backgroundColor);
    color: var(--foregroundColor);
  }
</style>
<h1>Hello</h1>
```

The `<style>` block can also reference any CSS variable already available on the page.

Content collections provide performant, content-focused APIs for loading multiple files instead of `import.meta.glob()`, so prefer `getCollection()` for structured content like blog posts, and reserve `import.meta.glob()` for importing arbitrary sets of local files (e.g. components) that aren't managed as a collection. Query with `const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");` (imported from `astro:content`). The returned shape differs: frontmatter values live on `data` instead of `frontmatter`, and each entry has an `id`/slug rather than a full `url`, so you construct paths yourself:

```astro
---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const allPosts = await getCollection("blog");
---
{allPosts.map((post) => <a href={`/posts/${post.id}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>)}
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/imports/

Apply `writing-mode: vertical-rl` with `text-orientation: mixed` on the paragraph, and set `lang="ja"` on the same element so the browser picks the Japanese line-break dictionary — CSS alone is not enough:

```astro
<p lang="ja" class="vertical">日本の段落</p>

<style>
  .vertical {
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    text-orientation: mixed;
  }
  :lang(ja) {
    line-break: strict;
    word-break: keep-all;
  }
</style>
```

`line-break: strict` enforces Japanese publisher conventions (JIS X 4051), and `word-break: keep-all` ensures wraps fall between characters rather than inside compounds.

Instantiate `Intl.PluralRules` in the component script and index a category-keyed message map in the template; Astro templates evaluate JavaScript expressions, so this works like any other frontmatter value.

```astro
---
const count = 5;
const rules = new Intl.PluralRules("ru");
const messages = {
  one: `${count} файл`,
  few: `${count} файла`,
  many: `${count} файлов`,
  other: `${count} файла`,
};
const label = messages[rules.select(count)];
---
<p>{label}</p>
```

`select()` returns a CLDR category (`zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many`, `other`), so store strings by category rather than hard-coding `count === 1` logic, which breaks for most locales (Japanese and Chinese use only `other`). Pass `{ type: "ordinal" }` for "1st/2nd/3rd", and pair with `Intl.NumberFormat` for locale-appropriate digits.

Configure `image.domains` for an exact allowlist and/or `image.remotePatterns` for pattern-based allowlists; remote URLs matching neither are not optimized (and raise the "Remote image is not allowed" error when used).

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  image: {
    domains: ['astro.build'],
    remotePatterns: [{ protocol: 'https', hostname: '**.amazonaws.com' }],
  },
});
```

`domains` takes exact domain strings only — no wildcards; use `remotePatterns` (with `protocol`, `hostname`, `port`, `pathname`) when you need them. Hostname wildcards: `**.` allows all subdomains, `*.` one level; pathname `/**` allows all sub-routes, `/*` one level. Redirects are followed, so the final destination must also be allowed. Unauthorized images still render via `<Image />`, avoiding CLS, just without optimization.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Set both attributes on each switcher link to the locale that link points to: `lang` declares the language of the link's own text, so a screen reader switches voice and announces "Deutsch" correctly even on a page in another language, while `hreflang` tells search engines and browsers which language the target URL serves. Combine this with a native-language label and a translated path:

```astro
---
import { languages } from "../i18n/ui";
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from "../i18n/utils";
const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);
---
<ul>
  {Object.entries(languages).map(([lang, label]) => (
    <li>
      <a href={translatePath("/", lang)} lang={lang} hreflang={lang}>{label}</a>
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
```

Add a region to the label only where it disambiguates, e.g. "English (UK)" vs "English (US)".

Use `Intl.PluralRules` with `{ type: "ordinal" }` — ordinals follow a different CLDR rule set from cardinals — and store strings per plural category (`one`, `two`, `few`, `other`, …) rather than per number, letting `select()` pick the form. Pair it with `Intl.NumberFormat` so digits render for the locale:

```js
const ord = new Intl.PluralRules("en", { type: "ordinal" });
const forms = { one: "{n}st", two: "{n}nd", few: "{n}rd", other: "{n}th" };
const text = forms[ord.select(n)].replace("{n}", new Intl.NumberFormat("en").format(n));
```

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/i18n/plural-rules/

Map each route per language and translate paths through helpers. Add route mappings in `src/i18n/ui.ts`:
```ts
export const routes = {
  de: { services: "leistungen" },
  fr: { services: "prestations-de-service" },
};
```
Then in `src/i18n/utils.ts` create `useTranslatedPath(lang)`, which looks up `routes[l][pathName]` for non-default languages and prefixes `/${l}` (omitting it for the default language when `showDefaultLang` is false). Use it in links: `const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang); <a href={translatePath("/services/")}>`, and pair it with `getRouteFromUrl(Astro.url)` in a `LanguagePicker` so switching languages keeps users on the equivalent translated route.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/recipes/i18n/

Call `Astro.redirect()` from the page's frontmatter and `return` it so the redirect fires before any HTML streams:

```astro
---
import { isLoggedIn } from "../utils";
const cookie = Astro.request.headers.get("cookie");
if (!isLoggedIn(cookie)) {
  return Astro.redirect("/login");
}
---
```

Because Astro streams HTML in on-demand rendering, the redirect must happen at the page level, not inside child components — a component-level redirect arrives too late. `Astro.redirect` is for request-dependent runtime decisions (auth, sessions) and requires on-demand rendering; in static mode routes are fixed at build time via `getStaticPaths()`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Follow the i18n recipe's "Hide default language in the URL" steps:

1. Keep default-language pages directly in `src/pages/` and put each other language in its own directory (e.g. `src/pages/fr/about.astro`).
2. In `src/i18n/ui.ts`, add `export const showDefaultLang = false;`
3. In `src/i18n/utils.ts`, add a path-translating helper:

```ts
import { ui, defaultLang, showDefaultLang } from "./ui";
export function useTranslatedPath(lang: keyof typeof ui) {
  return function translatePath(path: string, l: string = lang) {
    return !showDefaultLang && l === defaultLang ? path : `/${l}${path}`;
  };
}
```

4. Import `useTranslatedPath` in components and link with `translatePath("/home/")`.
5. Use it in `LanguagePicker.astro` with `translatePath("/", lang)` so the default language links to an unprefixed path.

Astro v4.0+ also ships built-in i18n routing as an alternative to this hand-rolled recipe.
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/internationalization/

Target the language with CSS's `:lang()` pseudo-class, which matches elements whose `lang` attribute (or an ancestor's) equals the given language—useful for typography and quotation marks. Declare `lang` on the elements (e.g. `<span lang="fr">` or `<html lang={lang}>` from `getLangFromUrl(Astro.url)`), then style them in a component `<style>` block:
```css
:lang(fr) { font-style: italic; }
:lang(de) { hyphens: auto; }
```
Scoped styles work normally here; use `is:global` or a global stylesheet if the language styling must span components.

`eagerness` accepts `'immediate' | 'eager' | 'moderate' | 'conservative'`, defaulting to `'immediate'` (added in `astro@5.6.0`), mirroring the Speculation Rules API from most to least eager. It is available when the experimental `clientPrerender` flag is enabled, which upgrades prefetching to client-side prerendering of link targets in supported browsers:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  prefetch: {
    prefetchAll: true,
    defaultStrategy: 'viewport',
  },
  experimental: {
    clientPrerender: true,
  },
});
```

Use `conservative` for resource-heavy pages and `moderate` for large link sets so the browser schedules them (FIFO); where the Speculation Rules API is unsupported, `prefetch` falls back to the supported strategy.

Astro does not automatically compress text resources; you must configure compression at the server or CDN level.

To compress text responses like HTML, CSS, JavaScript, JSON, SVG, XML, and plain text, use your hosting provider's settings:

- **Cloudflare**: Enable "Auto Minify" in the dashboard.
- **Fastly**: Configure compression rules via the Fastly UI or API.
- **AWS CloudFront**: Use Lambda@Edge functions to add `Content-Encoding` headers.

For self-hosted deployments (e.g., with a Node.js server), configure your server:

```js
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { brotliCompressSync, gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  const acceptEncoding = req.headers['accept-encoding'] || '';
  const encoding = acceptEncoding.includes('br') ? 'br' : 'gzip';
  const compress = encoding === 'br' ? brotliCompressSync : gzipSync;
  // ... serve compressed assets
});
```

Set `Vary: Accept-Encoding` to ensure proper caching by CDNs.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/compression/

Create a dynamic route file `src/pages/authors/[author].astro` and export `getStaticPaths()` returning one object per author with a `params` property; read the value from `Astro.params`.

```
---
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { author: "ada" }, props: { bio: "..." } },
    { params: { author: "grace" }, props: { bio: "..." } },
  ];
}
const { author } = Astro.params;
const { bio } = Astro.props;
---
<h1>{author}</h1>
<p>{bio}</p>
```

In default static output this generates `/authors/ada`, `/authors/grace`, etc. at build time, so every author must be predetermined. In SSR mode the page is generated on request for any matching route and `getStaticPaths()` is not required. Extra data (bio text) can be passed via `props`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/routing/

Listen for `pageshow` in a client-side `<script>` in your page and branch on `event.persisted`, which is `true` only when the page was restored from BFCache:

```
<script>
  window.addEventListener("pageshow", (event) => {
    if (event.persisted) {
      // restore state: reopen connections, refresh data
    }
  });
</script>
```

Pair it with `pagehide` for cleanup (never `unload`, which blocks BFCache), and reopen any WebSockets or IndexedDB transactions you closed when the page was hidden.

Docs: https://specification.website/spec/performance/bfcache/

To optimize fallback fonts in Astro and prevent layout shifts, use a generic font family like `sans-serif` or `monospace` as the fallback in your font configuration. Astro automatically generates optimized fallbacks from the last defined fallback if it's a generic font family. You can configure this behavior using the `fonts` option in `astro.config.mjs`, specifying `fallbacks` for each font.

Example:

```js
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";

export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [
    {
      provider: fontProviders.fontsource(),
      name: "Cousine",
      cssVariable: "--font-cousine",
      fallbacks: ["monospace"]
    }
  ]
});
```

If you want to disable automatic optimization, set `font.optimizedFallbacks` to `false`. Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/fonts/

Enable route caching (added in `astro@7.0.0`) by setting a cache provider in your Astro config, then control caching per request with `Astro.cache` in `.astro` pages or `context.cache` in API routes and middleware. Steps: (1) choose a provider — the built-in `memoryCache()`, or an experimental adapter CDN provider (`cacheNetlify()`, `cacheVercel()`, `cacheCloudflare()`) for Netlify/Vercel/Cloudflare; (2) set it in `defineConfig`; (3) set cache directives on your on-demand rendered pages and endpoints (`max-age`, `stale-while-revalidate`, tags), optionally declaring defaults for route groups via `routeRules`; (4) invalidate with `cache.invalidate({ path })`.

```js
import { defineConfig, memoryCache } from 'astro/config';
import node from '@astrojs/node';
export default defineConfig({
  adapter: node({ mode: 'standalone' }),
  cache: { provider: memoryCache() },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/caching/

Cache hints are declarative metadata returned by live loaders (`lastModified`, `tags`) that do not cause any caching by themselves; manually configured HTTP headers are the actual caching strategy applied to the response. Without a cache provider, you translate hints into headers yourself:

```astro
if (cacheHint?.tags) {
  Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Tag', cacheHint.tags.join(','));
}
if (cacheHint?.lastModified) {
  Astro.response.headers.set('Last-Modified', cacheHint.lastModified.toUTCString());
}
```

With a cache provider configured, you instead pass hints straight to route caching via `Astro.cache.set(cacheHint)` (collection hints merge: tags combine, `lastModified` takes the most recent value).

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Yes. Export both functions from the same endpoint file — each HTTP method is a separate named export (uppercase `POST`), and the file path defines the route, so one endpoint can respond differently per method:

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";

export const GET: APIRoute = () => {
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ method: "GET" }));
};

export const POST = (async ({ request }) => {
  const body = await request.json();
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(body), { status: 200 });
}) satisfies APIRoute;
```

For entirely different URLs, use separate files. In `static` mode, custom endpoints require `export const prerender = false`; in `server` mode they render on demand by default.

Gate every scroll-driven animation behind a `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` media query that turns it off, because the motion is yours rather than the browser's:

```css
.reveal {
  animation: fade-in linear both;
  animation-timeline: view();
  animation-range: entry 0% cover 30%;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .reveal { animation: none; }
}
```

With the animation removed under `reduce`, no viewport-driven transform or opacity motion plays for users susceptible to vestibular issues; content renders in its static state. Since scroll-driven animations are an `optional` feature — nothing breaks if omitted — this opt-in gating is required whenever the motion ships.

Astro treats CSS Modules like any other stylesheet — importing a `[name].module.css` file automatically applies its CSS to the page, while the default `styles` object maps original classnames to unique scoped identifiers — and inlining is decided per built chunk at build time, not per component. In production Astro minifies and combines CSS into per-page chunks (plus shared chunks split off for reuse), inlining any chunk under 4 kB into `<style type="text/css">` and linking larger ones as `<link rel="stylesheet">`. Tune the threshold with Vite's `assetsInlineLimit`, or force the behavior with `build.inlineStylesheets`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    inlineStylesheets: 'never'
  }
});
```

Use `'always'` to inline every stylesheet.

In Astro v7, the default `compressHTML` behavior changed from `true` (HTML-aware compression) to `'jsx'` (whitespace stripping using JSX rules), which may affect how inline elements are rendered. To prevent visual issues, inspect your site and explicitly add spaces between inline elements using `{" "}` if needed.

To restore the previous behavior, set `compressHTML: true` in your Astro configuration:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  compressHTML: true,
});
```

Alternatively, disable compression entirely with `compressHTML: false`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v7/

Set `build.inlineStylesheets: 'always'` in `astro.config.mjs` to inline all project stylesheets into `<style>` tags instead of external files. The default `'auto'` only inlines stylesheets under 4kb; `'never'` uses external stylesheets exclusively.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  build: { inlineStylesheets: 'always' },
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Call `render(entry)` from `astro:content` to get `remarkPluginFrontmatter`, then read `lastModified` from it and format it before rendering:

```astro
---
import { getCollection, render } from "astro:content";
import dayjs from "dayjs";
import utc from "dayjs/plugin/utc";

dayjs.extend(utc);

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const blog = await getCollection("blog");
  return blog.map((entry) => ({ params: { slug: entry.id }, props: { entry } }));
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { Content, remarkPluginFrontmatter } = await render(entry);
const lastModified = dayjs(remarkPluginFrontmatter.lastModified)
  .utc()
  .format("HH:mm:ss DD MMMM YYYY UTC");
---
<p>Last Modified: {lastModified}</p>
<Content />
```

The property only exists if a remark plugin (e.g. `remarkModifiedTime`) is registered via `markdown.processor` in `astro.config.mjs`. With a Markdown layout instead, read `Astro.props.frontmatter.lastModified`. Git-based times may be inaccurate on hosts that do shallow clones.

Import `prefetch()` from the `astro:prefetch` module in a client-side script and call it from the click handler:

```html
<button id="btn">Click me</button>
<script>
  import { prefetch } from 'astro:prefetch';
  const btn = document.getElementById('btn');
  btn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    prefetch('/about');
  });
</script>
```

`prefetch()` includes data saver mode (`saveData`) and slow connection (`effectiveType`) detection, so it only prefetches when needed. To bypass that detection, pass `prefetch('/about', { ignoreSlowConnection: true })`. Import `prefetch()` only in client-side scripts, since it relies on browser APIs.

Implement a custom live loader by exporting a `LiveLoader` from `astro/loaders` with `loadCollection` and `loadEntry` methods that return `cacheHint` objects containing `tags` and `lastModified`. In your pages, retrieve the cache hint via `getLiveEntry` and pass it to `Astro.cache.set(cacheHint)` if using a cache provider, or manually set response headers like `Cache-Tag` and `Last-Modified` from the hint.

```ts
// my-loader.ts
import type { LiveLoader } from "astro/loaders";
export function myLoader(config: { apiKey: string }): LiveLoader {
  return {
    name: "cached-loader",
    loadCollection: async ({ filter }) => {
      const products = await loadStoreProducts(filter);
      return {
        entries: products.map(item => ({
          id: item.id,
          data: item,
          cacheHint: { tags: [`product-${item.id}`, `category-${item.category}`] }
        })),
        cacheHint: { lastModified: getLastModifiedDate(products), tags: ["products"] }
      };
    },
    loadEntry: async ({ filter }) => {
      const item = await loadStoreProduct(filter);
      return { id: item.id, data: item, cacheHint: { lastModified: new Date(item.lastModified), tags: [`product-${item.id}`, `category-${item.category}`] } };
    }
  };
}
```

```astro
// src/pages/store/[id].astro
---
export const prerender = false;
import { getLiveEntry } from 'astro:content';
const { entry, error, cacheHint } = await getLiveEntry('products', Astro.params.id);
if (error) return Astro.redirect('/404');
if (cacheHint) Astro.cache.set(cacheHint);
---
<h1>{entry.data.name}</h1>
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/content-loader-reference/

Set `compressHTML: true` in `astro.config.mjs` — this removes whitespace from `.astro` components in a lossless manner, preserving some whitespace where needed for visual rendering:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  compressHTML: true
});
```

Note that since v7.0 the default changed from `true` to `'jsx'`, which applies React-style whitespace rules instead; `compressHTML: false` disables compression entirely.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

Register fonts with the `fonts` option in `astro.config.mjs`; each entry requires a `provider` from the `fontProviders` helper, a `name`, and a `cssVariable` that exposes the font to your CSS. For a local file, add it under `src/` (e.g. `src/assets/fonts/`) and configure it with the local provider:

```astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig, fontProviders } from "astro/config";
export default defineConfig({
  fonts: [{
    provider: fontProviders.local(),
    name: "DistantGalaxy",
    cssVariable: "--font-distant-galaxy",
    options: {
      variants: [{
        src: ['./src/assets/fonts/DistantGalaxy.woff2'],
        weight: 'normal',
        style: 'normal'
      }]
    }
  }]
});
```

Built-in providers cover Adobe, Bunny, Fontshare, Fontsource, Google, Google Icons, and NPM. For variable fonts, pass the weight range instead of individual weights (e.g. `weights: ["300 700"]`, or `weight: "100 900"` for a local variant). Once configured, add the font to your page head to use it.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/fonts/

`getStaticPaths()` must return an array of route objects: each object **requires** a `params` object whose keys match the bracket parameters in the file path (values are URL-encoded, so strings only), and may **optionally** include a `props` object whose data is passed to the page template via `Astro.props`.

```js
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { post: '1' } },
    { params: { post: '2' }, props: { /* extra data */ } },
  ];
}
```

In static/SSG mode omitting the export throws `GetStaticPathsRequired`; in `server` output mode dynamic routes don't need it unless you opt into prerendering with `export const prerender = true`. The function runs once in an isolated scope, so only file imports from the parent scope are accessible.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/routing-reference/

Return the response from `next()` in your middleware, then set the headers on it before returning:

```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const response = await next();
  response.headers.set("Deprecation", "@1717200000");
  response.headers.set(
    "Sunset",
    "Tue, 30 Jun 2026 23:59:59 GMT",
  );
  response.headers.set(
    "Link",
    '<https://api.example.com/v2/orders>; rel="successor-version"',
  );
  return response;
});
```

`Deprecation` takes an `@`-prefixed epoch-seconds date (past = already deprecated); `Sunset` takes an HTTP date and must not be earlier than `Deprecation`. Keep serving the resource normally until the Sunset date, then return `410 Gone`. Middleware must always return a `Response`, directly or from `next()`. These headers are emitted only for on-demand routes — prerendered pages run middleware at build time.

Add the two Trusted Types directives to `security.csp.directives` in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: {
      directives: [
        "require-trusted-types-for 'script'",
        "trusted-types escape"
      ]
    }
  }
});
```

`security.csp.directives` (added in `astro@6.0.0`) takes an `Array<string>` of directives beyond the default `script-src` and `style-src`, and they are added to all pages; after the build they appear in a `<meta http-equiv="content-security-policy">` element alongside Astro's defaults. The `trusted-types` value must list the policy names your page creates (here `escape`). Integrations can read the same list via `SSRManifest.csp.directives`.

Set `security.csp.scriptDirective.strictDynamic` to `true` to enable the `strict-dynamic` keyword (boolean, default `false`, added in `astro@6.0.0`):

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: {
    csp: {
      scriptDirective: {
        strictDynamic: true
      }
    }
  }
});
```

This applies to `script-src`. When you also scope `script` resources or hashes to `script-src-elem` (using `kind: "element"`), `strict-dynamic` is inherited by `script-src-elem` so dynamically injected `<script>` elements continue to work.

To configure an Astro endpoint to handle a specific HTTP method like `POST`, export a function named after that method (e.g., `POST`) from your `.ts` or `.js` file in the `src/pages/` directory. The exported function must be typed as `APIRoute`.

Example:
```ts
// src/pages/form-submit.ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro';

export const POST: APIRoute = ({ request }) => {
  // Handle form submission logic here
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'Form submitted!' }));
};
```

This approach aligns with Astro’s requirement that HTTP methods be uppercase, as of v3.0 and enforced in v4.0.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/endpoints/

Enable it with the top-level `security.csp` option (added in Astro 6, default `false`) in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
export default defineConfig({
  security: { csp: true },
});
```

Astro then injects a `<meta http-equiv="content-security-policy">` element into each page's `<head>` whose `content` supplies `script-src` and `style-src` hashes based on the scripts and styles that page uses. Pass an object instead of `true` to set `algorithm` (`'SHA-256'` default, `'SHA-384'`, `'SHA-512'`) or add extra directives via `directives`. CSP is not supported in `dev` mode (Vite dev server); test with `build` and `preview`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/configuration-reference/

In Astro 7, actions are public endpoints by default and must be secured with explicit authorization checks in their handlers or via middleware. Authorization within action handlers ensures fine-grained control per action, including user session validation and permission logic, using `context.locals` and throwing `ActionError` for unauthorized access.

Middleware-based gating applies to all actions (or a subset) globally before they are invoked, enforcing session presence but not handling specific action permissions or rate-limiting. It's useful for basic access control like requiring a valid session cookie, but doesn't replace the need for per-action authorization logic in handlers.

Example of handler-level auth:
```ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from 'astro:actions';
export const server = {
  getUserSettings: defineAction({
    handler: async (_input, context) => {
      if (!context.locals.user) {
        throw new ActionError({ code: 'UNAUTHORIZED' });
      }
      return { /* data */ };
    }
  })
}
```

Example of middleware-level gating:
```ts
import { defineMiddleware } from "astro:middleware";
import { getActionContext } from "astro:actions";

export const onRequest = defineMiddleware(async (context, next) => {
  const { action } = getActionContext(context);
  if (action?.calledFrom === "rpc" && !context.cookies.has("user-session")) {
    return new Response("Forbidden", { status: 403 });
  }
  return next();
});
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/actions/

Create an Astro endpoint that exports a POST handler and return a `Response` object; browsers POST Reporting API JSON to the named endpoints declared in `Reporting-Endpoints`.

```ts
import type { APIRoute } from 'astro'

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request }) => {
  const report = await request.json()
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ ok: true }), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  })
}
```

This requires SSR (`output: 'server'`) with an adapter, since endpoints only run on demand. Returning a simple object is deprecated since Astro v3; return `Response` directly. Point the header at your collector (e.g. `Reporting-Endpoints: csp-endpoint="/reports", default="/reports"`), typically configured via platform headers such as `public/_headers`. CSP references the endpoint name with `report-to`, not `report-uri`.

Astro injects heading IDs **after** your custom plugins run, so any `id` your plugin sets is preserved and overrides Astro's default — no conflict. If a later plugin needs to read Astro's generated IDs, import Astro's own heading-ID plugin and place it before those plugins:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified, rehypeHeadingIds } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import { otherPluginThatReliesOnHeadingIDs } from 'some/plugin/source';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      rehypePlugins: [rehypeHeadingIds, otherPluginThatReliesOnHeadingIDs],
    }),
  },
});
```

For the Sätteri processor, use `satteriHeadingIdsPlugin` from `@astrojs/markdown-satteri` the same way.

Intercept the path in `src/middleware.js` and return a 302 (or 303) redirect to your real change-password page:

```js
export function onRequest(context, next) {
  if (context.url.pathname === '/.well-known/change-password') {
    return new Response(null, {
      status: 302,
      headers: { Location: '/account/security/password' },
    });
  }
  return next();
}
```

Serve it over HTTPS on the canonical host, point it at the page for changing an *existing* password (not the forgotten-password reset flow, and not sign-up), and make that destination handle signed-out users by sending them to sign in first. Never return 404 for this URL — it tells password managers the feature is unsupported. Middleware for prerendered pages runs at build time, so this route must be rendered on demand for the redirect to be served per request. Only implement this if your site actually has user accounts.

Hardcoding tag routes means every new tag added to a post requires revisiting and editing `[tag].astro`, so routes drift out of sync with your content. Instead, derive tags from frontmatter inside `getStaticPaths()` and map them to routes, filtering posts before passing them as props:

```astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true }));
  const uniqueTags = [...new Set(allPosts.map((post: any) => post.frontmatter.tags).flat())];
  return uniqueTags.map((tag) => ({
    params: { tag },
    props: { posts: allPosts.filter((post: any) => post.frontmatter.tags.includes(tag)) },
  }));
}
const { tag } = Astro.params;
const { posts } = Astro.props;
---
```

Each tag automatically gets its own page, and the template can map over `posts` directly.

Run `npx astro add preact` (or `pnpm astro add preact` / `yarn astro add preact`) from your project directory and follow the prompts; this installs `@astrojs/preact` and wires up the config for you. For a manual install, run `npm install @astrojs/preact` (also install `preact` if you see a `Cannot find package 'preact'` warning), then register it in `astro.config.*`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import preact from '@astrojs/preact';

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [preact()],
});
```

Also add `"jsx": "react-jsx"` and `"jsxImportSource": "preact"` to `compilerOptions` in `tsconfig.json`.

Set `title` and `description` as options to the `rss()` helper in your feed endpoint `src/pages/rss.xml.js`; language has no dedicated option, so inject it via `customData`:

```js
import rss, { pagesGlobToRssItems } from '@astrojs/rss';

export async function GET(context) {
  return rss({
    title: 'Astro Learner | Blog',
    description: 'My journey learning Astro',
    site: context.site,
    items: await pagesGlobToRssItems(import.meta.glob('./**/*.md')),
    customData: `<language>en-us</language>`,
  });
}
```

Absolute article links come from `site`, which you set in `astro.config.mjs`.

`getStaticPaths()` is a function (added in `astro@1.0.0`, type `(options: GetStaticPathsOptions) => Promise<GetStaticPathsResult> | GetStaticPathsResult`) that generates multiple prerendered routes from a single `.astro` file with parameters in its path, for build-time (static) rendering. It returns an array of objects: required `params` (keys matching the file's parameters, string values) and optional `props` (any data, read in the template via `Astro.props`). Generate pages from Markdown like this:

```astro
---
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const allPosts = Object.values(import.meta.glob('../posts/*.md', { eager: true }));
  return allPosts.map((post: any) => ({
    params: { post: post.file.split('/').pop().replace('.md', '') },
    props: { post },
  }));
}
const { post } = Astro.props;
---
```

Notes: it runs once in an isolated scope before any page loads (only file imports may be referenced), it also works in static endpoints, and in `server` mode you must opt in with `export const prerender = true`.

Await them as methods instead of accessing them as properties:

```js
// Before (Astro 5)
const mappings = manifest.serverIslandMappings;
const driver = manifest.sessionDriver;

// After (Astro 6)
const mappings = await manifest.serverIslandMappings?.();
const driver = await manifest.sessionDriver?.();
```

`serverIslandMappings` is typed as `() => Promise<ServerIslandMappings> | ServerIslandMappings` and `sessionDriver()` as `() => Promise<{ default: SessionDriverFactory | null }>`, both added in `astro@6.0.0`.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v6/

In Astro 6, access non-public variables in your code with `import.meta.env` (e.g. `import.meta.env.DB_PASSWORD`), not `process.env` — Astro does not rewrite them to `process.env` references. Non-public variables are server-side only: they cannot appear in client code at all; only `PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables reach the browser. Under SSR they resolve at runtime through the adapter (most use `process.env`, Deno uses `Deno.env.get()`), checking the server environment before falling back to `.env` files.

```js
// server-side only
const data = await db(import.meta.env.DB_PASSWORD);
```

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/environment-variables/

Install `@astrojs/markdown-remark` (it is no longer installed by default in Astro 7), then set `unified()` as your Markdown processor in `astro.config.mjs`:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
import { unified } from '@astrojs/markdown-remark';
import remarkImgAttr from 'remark-imgattr';
import rehypeGithubAlerts from 'rehype-github-alerts';

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    processor: unified({
      remarkPlugins: [
        [remarkImgAttr, { defaults: { width: 700, format: 'avif' } }],
      ],
      rehypePlugins: [rehypeGithubAlerts],
    }),
  },
});
```

Options are passed as a nested array: `[plugin, optionsObject]`. The deprecated `markdown.remarkPlugins`, `markdown.rehypePlugins`, and `markdown.remarkRehype` options also still work, but they too now require `@astrojs/markdown-remark` to be installed.

Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/markdown-content/

Astro 6 deprecates the string driver signature; instead build the driver with the `sessionDrivers` export (added in `astro@5.7.0`) and assign it to `session.driver`, with `cookie` and `ttl` as sibling keys of `driver` inside `session`:

```js
import { defineConfig, sessionDrivers } from 'astro/config'
export default defineConfig({
  session: {
    driver: sessionDrivers.redis({
      url: process.env.REDIS_URL
    }),
    cookie: {
      secure: true
    },
    ttl: 3600
  }
})
```

The driver options (`url`) move inside the `sessionDrivers.redis()` call rather than living in a separate `options` object; `cookie` and `ttl` remain top-level `session` options.

Import Zod from `"astro/zod"` instead of `"astro:content"`, which no longer exports `z`:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { glob, file } from 'astro/loaders';

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ base: './src/content/blog', pattern: '**/*.{md,mdx}' }),
  schema: z.object({ title: z.string() }),
});
```

`astro/zod` is a re-export of the Zod library and supports all Zod 4 features, so `z.object()`, `z.coerce.date()`, `.transform()`, and the rest of your schema work unchanged in `src/content.config.ts`.

Replace `import.meta.env.ASSETS_PREFIX` with `build.assetsPrefix` imported from `astro:config/server`. This is a drop-in replacement.

```js
import { build } from "astro:config/server";
someLogic(build.assetsPrefix);
```
Docs: https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v6/

Astro 6.0 deprecated `astro:schema` and the `z` re-export from `astro:content`, consolidating everything on the `astro/zod` export. Two changes:

1. Replace `import { z } from "astro:schema"` with `import { z } from "astro/zod"`.
2. Remove `z` from your `astro:content` import and import it separately:

```ts
import { defineCollection } from "astro:content";
import { z } from "astro/zod";
import { glob, file } from "astro/loaders";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.md", base: "./src/data/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({ title: z.string(), pubDate: z.coerce.date() }),
});
```

`astro/zod` is a re-export of Zod supporting all Zod 4 features, so schema usage is unchanged.

Compare `event.type` against the string literal `'astro:before-preparation'`, e.g.:

```js
document.addEventListener('astro:before-preparation', (event) => {
  if (event.type === 'astro:before-preparation') {
    const originalLoader = event.loader;
  }
});
```

The lifecycle event names are `astro:before-preparation`, `astro:after-preparation`, `astro:before-swap`, `astro:after-swap`, and `astro:page-load`, all fired on `document`. `TRANSITION_BEFORE_PREPARATION` and the `isTransitionBeforePreparationEvent()` helper are no longer importable from `astro:transitions/client`; the event name itself is the check.
